CG.CONTENT = {"articles":[],"rulebook":{"chapters":[{"num":0,"title":"How our season works — and why","sections":[{"id":"0.1","title":"Reading this chapter","paragraphs":["This chapter describes how a CGHL season is conducted, in order, together with the purpose of each stage. It is intended to be read in full before a member's first game. The binding rules are set out in the chapters that follow; where a stage described here carries a requirement, the rule that governs it is cited.","The league conducts each season under one of two season formats, designated by the commissioner for that season and stated at the head of this rulebook. The basic format is the league standard and is the text of these chapters: a season with no pre-season, a single draft night, six (6) game-weeks, a three-round postseason, and every player returned to the draft pool at its end. The full format is the more extensive model — a pre-season, a free-agency period, multi-season contracts, contract extensions and the trading of draft picks — held in reserve for a larger league; its provisions are preserved in full in Appendix A and are not in force while the basic format is designated. Where the two formats differ, the chapters that follow state the provision in force and the appendix carries the alternative.","Two figures are season settings rather than fixed provisions of this rulebook: the size and composition of the active roster (Rule 2.1) and the number of rounds in the entry draft (Rule 2.8). The commissioner determines both for each season and publishes them to the clubs before that season's draft; once published for a season they are binding for its duration. The figures in force appear on the site — on the Seasons page, in the draft room and in Team HQ — and the office announces them when they are set.","The calendar is spaced from a single anchor, draft night, and every later date follows from it, so that the dates are internally consistent and every club knows what follows."]},{"id":"0.2","title":"Step one — the off-season","paragraphs":["The basic format has no formal off-season. Registration for a season opens automatically when the previous season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4), and the interval between a season's final and the next season's draft is used by the league office to renew or appoint each club's Owner and by those Owners to seat their General Manager and Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6). No club may draft until all three seats are held (Rule 2.8).","The interval is short by design. Because every roster is rebuilt through the draft and no player contract carries from one season to the next, the only precondition for a new season is that each club's front office is in place."],"full":["The off-season begins at the first midnight after the previous season's final playoff game and runs two full weeks with no games on the ice. These two weeks exist for the league office to renew or appoint each club's Owner, and for those Owners to set their management staff, before the pre-season opens — next-season sign-ups have been open since the previous season’s movement deadline (Rule 1.1).","Why it comes first: rosters are rebuilt every season through the draft and the contracts that carry over — including extensions a club signed during its players' final season (Rule 2.5) — so the people who will run the draft — the Owners and their managers — have to be in place before anyone signs up. The two dark weeks give the office room to seat clubs deliberately rather than under game-night pressure."]},{"id":"0.3","title":"Step two — registration and the eligibility deadline","paragraphs":["Registration for a season opens when the previous season's movement deadline passes (Rule 1.1); for the league's inaugural season it opened at launch. The sign-up cutoff — 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding the draft — is a draft-eligibility cutoff and not a closure of registration. A player registered by the cutoff enters the draft. A player who registers after it, at any point in the season, remains eligible to play and is placed on a club by the league office under Rule 2.8 rather than drafted.","Registration remains open so that a player who discovers the league late is not excluded. Automatic placement under Rule 2.8 assigns each late registrant to a club as depth, at the league minimum salary, until placement closes at the movement deadline (Rule 2.4). A player who registers after the movement deadline enters the following season's pool.","Until a season's registration ends, a registered player may withdraw his own registration from the Register page (Rule 1.1); a registered player who leaves the league Discord is withdrawn automatically after one day's grace (Rule 1.1). A player who has been placed on a club leaves the season only through the league office.","The position declared at registration governs the position at which a player is drafted and, if undrafted, placed. It may be changed only by request to the league office filed before the sign-up cutoff (Rule 2.9); after the cutoff it is fixed for the season."],"full":["Registration for a season opens the moment the previous season’s movement deadline passes (Rule 1.1) — for the league’s first season, during the off-season. The date the league calls the sign-up deadline — 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft — is a draft-eligibility cutoff, not a hard close: register on time and you enter the pre-season and the draft as a normal prospect; register after it — or join at any point later in the season — and you still play, you are simply placed on a club automatically rather than drafted.","Why it works this way: a league that slams registration shut turns away players who find it a week late. Registration stays open, and automatic placement under Rule 2.2 routes an unrostered arrival onto a club that has room, at the league minimum — up until random placement closes at the movement deadline (Rule 2.4). Register after that and you enter the next season’s pool.","The deadline has one more job: it is the measuring date for contracts. A player on a multi-year deal must still register — every season, without exception. Sign up and the contract puts the player straight back on the club; fail to sign up and the salary sits on the club's cap as dead money while the player sits out — and if the club has changed owners, the deal is voided and the player is suspended for its remaining seasons (Rule 2.5).","Until a season's registration ends, a registered player may withdraw their own sign-up from the Register page (Rule 1.1); leaving the league Discord does the same thing automatically after a day's grace (Rule 1.1). Once placed on a club, leaving goes through the league office.","One thing to get right at sign-up: the position you register at. It decides which club you are assigned to for the pre-season and how clubs value you in the draft, and it can only be changed by request to the league office up to 11:59 PM Eastern on the Tuesday before the first pre-season game (Rule 2.9). After that it is set for the season."]},{"id":"0.4","title":"Step three — draft week","paragraphs":["The basic format has no pre-season. No player is assigned to a club before the draft, no appearance requirement applies, and the interval between the sign-up cutoff and draft night is used by club management to prepare draft boards against the published pool (Rule 2.8) and by the league office to settle the pool. Every player registered by the cutoff is draft-eligible without further condition.","A pre-season occupies two game-weeks and conditions draft eligibility on an appearance count. In a developing league the season is better served by drafting every registered player on the published Saturday and opening the regular season on the following Wednesday. Overall ratings are compiled from regular-season play, so no information the pre-season provided is lost."],"full":["The pre-season opens on the first Wednesday after the two dark weeks end and runs two weeks; sign-ups stay open right through it, until 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft (Rule 1.1). A club’s management group plays for its own club, and in pre-season games its Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position, so that a club can fill its bench and get as many players into games as possible (Rule 5.2). Every other registered player is assigned randomly and evenly across all clubs for two weeks of real games that carry their own standings, separate from the regular season. The assignment fills the clubs' active rosters first, by position group (Rule 2.1): a player keeps his registered position and goes to a club with room in his group — a club short of left defensemen takes an extra right defenseman, a club short of centers an extra wing — the emptiest groups first; when no club has room in his group, he is loaned into another group's open seat — a forward may spend the pre-season on defense — so that every club ices a full roster before anyone is a spare. Once every active seat is filled, the remaining players are loaned out too — spread evenly across the clubs, at random — so every registered player has a club for the pre-season; there is no cap on how many pre-season loans a club carries. A pre-season loan does not change a player's registered position for the draft, counts against no roster limit, and returns to the draft pool when the pre-season ends.","The pre-season does two jobs at once, which is why it exists. It lets every player — new and returning — shake off rust in real games before anything counts; and its table is available to the league office as one basis for the draft order (Rule 2.8) — though a first season draws its order at random, and from the second season on the default is a lottery weighted by the previous regular season. Pre-season games count toward a player’s overall rating but never toward the regular-season standings or the playoff race.","The pre-season also decides who is in the draft. A player who is randomly assigned for the pre-season, and who is not already carried on a club’s full-time roster, must appear in at least three (3) pre-season games to remain draft-eligible (Rule 2.8). Returning players and club management are exempt and may play as much or as little of the pre-season as they wish. Because a player cannot reach three games on his own, club management is obligated to spread pre-season ice time as widely as it can, so that every player who needs the three games has a genuine chance to get them — a Thursday sign-up can still play the final Friday’s three games and make it."],"fullTitle":"Step three — the pre-season"},{"id":"0.5","title":"Step four — the draft","paragraphs":["Draft night is the Saturday following the sign-up cutoff, conducted live on the site at 7:00 PM Eastern Time. Each club rebuilds its roster by selecting from the pool of players registered by the cutoff, over the number of rounds published for the season (Rule 2.8), in a snake order: the drawn order is followed in the first round, reversed in the second, and alternated thereafter, so that the club selecting last in the first round selects first in the second. The salary of a drafted player is fixed by the round of his selection rather than negotiated — the final round pays the league minimum and each round above it adds one increment — so an earlier selection carries a greater cap hit (Rule 2.8).","The order is drawn at random for each season and bears no relation to the previous season's standings, playoffs or champion; the snake pattern offsets the advantage of an early selection. Each club maintains a private draft board in advance. If a club's clock expires during the draft, the league office selects the highest-ranked available player on that club's own board, so that an absent manager neither forfeits a selection nor receives a player the club did not rank."],"full":["The Saturday after the pre-season's final night is draft night, held live on the site at 7:00 PM Eastern. Clubs rebuild their rosters by selecting from the pool of eligible players in a fixed order that holds for every round — first overall picks first in each of the fourteen rounds — under the order and format the commissioner sets that season (Rule 2.8). What a drafted player earns is set by the round he goes in rather than negotiated — the last round pays the league minimum and every round above it adds a step — so picking earlier costs a club more against its cap (Rule 2.8).","Why a draft at all, and why the order favors last season’s weakest clubs — by default through an NHL-style lottery: drafting redistributes the season's talent so that no club stockpiles a roster across seasons and the weakest teams get first crack at improving. Clubs prepare a private draft board in advance; if a club's clock ever runs out on the night, the league drafts the best available player from that club's own board automatically, so an absent manager never costs his team a pick and never lands a player the club did not rank."]},{"id":"0.6","title":"Step five — placement and puck drop","paragraphs":["Every player registered by the cutoff is draft-eligible (Rule 2.8). Ten (10) minutes after the draft concludes, every registered player who remains without a club is placed on one by the league office, in random order, at the league minimum salary, as depth — a contract for the season on a club selected for him, which does not count against the club's roster composition (Rule 2.8). No club selects, and no registered player is left without a club. The basic format has no free-agency period: puck drop is the Wednesday following the draft, and the only signing available to a club during the season is of a player who has been waived, from the conclusion of the draft until the movement deadline (Rule 2.2).","A free-agency period exists to price players whose contracts have ended. Under the basic format every contract ends with its season and every player re-enters the draft, so there is no market to conduct."],"full":["Every player who registered by the deadline and met the three-game pre-season requirement is draft-eligible (Rule 2.8), so the draft covers the on-time pool. Ten minutes after the draft concludes, every registered player still without a club — undrafted, or short of five games — is placed on one by the league office at the league minimum, in random order, where a club has room in his position group (Rule 2.8). No club chooses, and no rookie goes to free agency. Free agency opens about a day after the draft and runs one full week, and it is for players whose contracts have ended (Rule 2.2): a player in the last season of his deal may talk only to the club that holds it — at any point in that season, and on until the free agency that follows it opens — and to any club once it has (Rule 2.2). In a first season nobody's contract has ended, so free agency is quiet; from the second season on it is where returning players move. The regular season does not start until free agency closes, so every club begins game one with a settled roster."],"fullTitle":"Step five — rookie placement and free agency"},{"id":"0.7","title":"Step six — the regular season","paragraphs":["The regular season begins on the Wednesday following the draft and is played on the shape published in Rule 3.1 — six (6) game-weeks, three nights a week, three games a night, fifty-four (54) games per club. A game-week containing a league-observed holiday is skipped in full and the schedule resumes the following week; the commissioner designates each season's observed holidays in the Control Center. Every active-roster player may be dressed in up to six (6) games a week and a training-camp player in up to three (3) (Rule 5.2); there is no weekly availability quota. The movement deadline falls at the close of the fourth game-week and freezes club-initiated signings, trades and automatic placement (Rule 2.4), so that the final two weeks are contested with settled rosters.","Every game produces a winner. A win in regulation or overtime is worth two (2) points; a loss in overtime is worth one (1); a loss in regulation is worth none. There are no ties and no shootouts (Rule 4.1). Clubs are ranked by total points earned (Rule 8.1), without adjustment for games in hand. A forfeit is scored for points as an ordinary result: two points to the club awarded the win and none to the club that forfeited."],"full":["The regular season starts the Wednesday after free agency closes, on the shape published in Rule 3.1 — the number of weeks, nights and games per club is set season by season and stated there. Game weeks holding a league-observed holiday are skipped entirely, so no one is asked to play on a holiday; the commissioner chooses each season’s observed holidays in the Control Center. Partway through the season a movement deadline freezes club-initiated signings, trades, and new-player placement (Rule 2.4), so the stretch run is contested with settled rosters.","Every game produces a winner. A win in regulation or overtime is worth two points; a loss in overtime is worth one; a loss in regulation is worth none — there are no ties and no shootouts (Rule 4.1). Clubs are ranked by total points earned (Rule 8.1). Games in hand are not adjusted for: a club that has played more games may sit higher on points alone, and the club beneath it carries the chance to pass it by playing its own. A forfeit is scored for points exactly as an ordinary result, two points to the winner and none to the club that forfeited. Every night, every point pushes toward the one thing the regular season decides: playoff position."]},{"id":"0.8","title":"Step seven — the playoffs","paragraphs":["The playoffs begin in the game-week immediately following the final regular-season week. Six (6) of the league's eight (8) clubs qualify — the top three (3) in each division — and the postseason is played over three (3) rounds in three (3) game-weeks (Chapter 8). In the first round the first seed in each division receives a bye while the second and third seeds meet; in the second round the winner meets the first seed for the division championship; in the third round the two division champions meet in the final. Each round is a best-of-seven series played within a single game-week: two games on Wednesday, two on Thursday and up to three on Friday. Seeding is fixed when the league office certifies the final standings and does not change between rounds.","Two rules keep the postseason honest. A player must have appeared in at least eighteen (18) regular-season games to be dressed in the playoffs, so that a club's postseason is played by the players who carried its regular season — Team HQ tracks every player against the floor through the season. And a playoff appearance cap of four (4) games per player per series applies to everyone, so that no line can carry a club alone (Rule 8.3)."],"full":["The playoffs open the game week immediately after the final regular-season week, so there is never a dark stretch or an overlap between the two. The top four clubs in each division qualify, an eight-club field, and play a divisional bracket to a champion (Chapter 8). Seeds are fixed when the office certifies the final standings and do not re-seed between rounds.","Two rules keep the postseason honest. Series are decided on depth: the weekly appearance cap applies to each series — a skater may be dressed in at most three (3) games of any single series and a goaltender in at most six (6) (Rule 8.3) — so one line cannot carry a club alone, and three rostered players at every skating position cover all seven games. There is no games-played floor: every rostered player is playoff-eligible."]},{"id":"0.9","title":"Step eight — champion and rollover","paragraphs":["The club that wins the final is declared champion, the result is entered in the permanent record, and the season is marked complete. Registration for the following season opens automatically when the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4), so that sign-ups for the next season are open while the current season concludes, and the next draft may be held as soon as every club's front office is seated.","Between seasons the league is reset. Every player contract expires with the season for which it was signed; no contract, extension or player right carries into the following season (Rule 2.5). Every player must register again, every registered player enters the next draft, and the order for that draft is drawn at random without reference to the previous season (Rule 2.8). Clubs and their front offices continue; rosters are rebuilt."],"full":["The club that wins the final is crowned champion, the result enters the permanent record, and the season is marked complete. That completion is what starts the clock on the next off-season — the first midnight after that final game — and the whole cycle begins again. One step now happens earlier than the rest: registration for the NEXT season opens automatically the moment the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4), so sign-ups for the following season are already running while the current one plays out.","Between seasons the league rolls over: contracts that reached the end of their term expire and return those players to the pool. Multi-year deals survive — but they no longer place anyone on a roster by themselves. Every player must register for the new season (Rule 2.5); registering returns a contracted player straight to the club, while an unsigned deal sits on the club's cap as dead money with the player unable to play. If the club's owner changed and the player never signed up, the deal is voided and the player suspended for its remaining term. Continuity for the clubs, a fresh draft for the league — and no roster spot handed to anyone who has not shown up for the season. A player whose club re-signed him during the exclusive window (Rule 2.5) is the exception: his new deal comes into force with the new season's cap year and registering returns him to that club; and a club keeps the exclusive right to re-sign a player whose deal just ended until the new season's free agency opens."]}]},{"num":1,"title":"Membership & conduct","sections":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Eligibility and registration","paragraphs":["Membership in the Chel Gaming Hockey League (CGHL) is open to players who complete registration through the league's official channels, link a valid EA account and gamertag, and join the league Discord server. A player is not eligible to appear in a CGHL game until the league office has confirmed their registration and assigned them to a club or to the free agent pool.","Each player may hold exactly one CGHL identity. Registering, playing under, or lending out a secondary account — including playing under another member's account for any reason — is account misrepresentation and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7. Players are responsible for keeping their gamertag and contact information current with the league office; games played under an unregistered or outdated gamertag may be ruled unofficial.","A registered player may withdraw their own sign-up from the Register page at any time until registration for that season ends. Withdrawal removes the player from the sign-up board and the draft pool; a withdrawn player may register again freely while registration remains open. Once a player has been drafted or placed on a club, leaving the season is a roster matter and goes through the league office.","Discord membership is not only an entry requirement — it holds for as long as the sign-up does. A registered player who leaves the league Discord before being placed on a club has their sign-up withdrawn automatically: once the player has been out of the server for about a day, the registration is removed from the sign-up board and the draft pool, archived by the league office, and the player is notified on the site. The day of grace exists so a brief absence — a rejoin minutes later, or a Discord hiccup — never costs anyone their sign-up. Rejoining the server restores nothing by itself; signing up again is a fresh registration and follows the normal draft-eligibility rules. Players already on a club roster are never touched by this rule — a rostered player who leaves the server is handled by the league office directly.","Registration for a season ends when the following season's registration opens, which occurs automatically when the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4); from that moment rosters are frozen and new registrations belong to the following season. Each season's sign-up cutoff is 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding that season's draft (Chapter 0, Step two); a player who registers after the cutoff is not excluded but is placed on a club as depth under Rule 2.8."],"full":["Membership in the Chel Gaming Hockey League (CGHL) is open to players who complete registration through the league's official channels, link a valid EA account and gamertag, and join the league Discord server. A player is not eligible to appear in a CGHL game until the league office has confirmed their registration and assigned them to a club or to the free agent pool.","Each player may hold exactly one CGHL identity. Registering, playing under, or lending out a secondary account — including playing under another member's account for any reason — is account misrepresentation and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7. Players are responsible for keeping their gamertag and contact information current with the league office; games played under an unregistered or outdated gamertag may be ruled unofficial.","A registered player may withdraw their own sign-up from the Register page at any time until registration for that season ends. Withdrawal removes the player from the sign-up board and the draft pool; a withdrawn player may register again freely while registration remains open. Once a player has been drafted or placed on a club, leaving the season is a roster matter and goes through the league office.","Discord membership is not only an entry requirement — it holds for as long as the sign-up does. A registered player who leaves the league Discord before being placed on a club has their sign-up withdrawn automatically: once the player has been out of the server for about a day, the registration is removed from the sign-up board and the draft pool, archived by the league office, and the player is notified on the site. The day of grace exists so a brief absence — a rejoin minutes later, or a Discord hiccup — never costs anyone their sign-up. Rejoining the server restores nothing by itself; signing up again is a fresh registration and follows the normal draft-eligibility rules. Players already on a club roster are never touched by this rule — a rostered player who leaves the server is handled by the league office directly.","Registration for a season ends when the following season's registration opens, which happens automatically when the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4) — from that moment, rosters are frozen and new sign-ups belong to the next season. Each season's draft-eligibility deadline is 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before that season's draft (Chapter 0, Step two); registering after it does not shut a player out, it places them on a club automatically under Rule 2.2."]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Code of conduct","paragraphs":["All members — players, club management, and league staff — are expected to compete hard and act like professionals. Harassment, hate speech, threats, doxxing, and targeted abuse are prohibited in all league spaces, including in-game chat, Discord, streams of league games, and direct messages arising out of league business. The standard applies whether or not the conduct occurs during a scheduled game.","Unsportsmanlike conduct includes, without limitation: deliberate goaltender interference intended to injure or provoke, quitting a game in progress without cause, stat manipulation, throwing games, and public abuse of officials or opponents. The league office may act on conduct violations on its own initiative; it does not require a formal complaint to open a review."]},{"id":"1.3","title":"League communications","paragraphs":["The CGHL Discord server is the league's official channel of record. Schedule releases, rule changes, disciplinary notices, and game codes are delivered through Discord, and every rostered player is responsible for information posted there. A claim of not having seen an official posting is not a defense to a missed obligation under this rulebook.","A club’s management — its Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6) — is the formal point of contact between the club and the league office. Protests, reschedule requests, and roster paperwork must come from the club’s management to be considered filed.","Server-wide pings — @everyone and @here — are reserved to the league office. Only the Commissioner and Staff roles can send them. No department role carries it, so a staffer whose only role is their department — media, for instance — asks the office to post. The permission is re-checked automatically, so a role that gains it by accident loses it again within minutes. Clubs reach their own members with their club role, and anyone who needs the whole league told something should ask the league office to post it. Inside its own club room a club’s management may mention whomever it needs, as often as it needs — the server’s spam filters never block the front office there.","Posting links, images, and file attachments in the Discord requires a league role — that is, an account that has signed in to the website and registered. Anyone may read and chat straight away — and may use Discord's built-in GIF picker, which stays open to everyone. The ability to post any other link or an image is granted upon registration, which completes within seconds; until then, a message containing a link is refused rather than posted. New accounts must also have been in the server ten (10) minutes before they can post at all. These limits exist to stop advertising bots, which join, post a link, and leave; they are not a judgment about any member. Advertising and scam content are blocked automatically and reported to the league office. Server invite links are treated no differently from any other link: a member who has signed up may post one, while an account that has not signed up may not. Club recruitment has its own home — #scouting-links is readable by the whole league, and only a club's Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager may post in it, so what appears there is a club's own invitation and not an open advertising board."]}]},{"num":2,"title":"Rosters & player movement","sections":[{"id":"2.1","title":"Roster composition","paragraphs":["The commissioner shall determine, for each season, the size of the active roster and its composition by position group — the number of forwards, defensemen and goaltenders — and shall publish these figures to the clubs before that season's draft. The composition may be published as a number of complete lines together with a number of additional players of any position, from which the league's roster tools derive the greatest number of forwards, defensemen and goaltenders a club may carry and the total it may not exceed. The figures published for a season are binding for its duration and are enforced by the league's roster tools. The size and composition in force appear on the Seasons page and in Team HQ.","Each club ices from an active roster shaped by position group. A forward may be dressed at center or at either wing, and the forward group may hold centers, left wings and right wings in any mix; a defenseman may be dressed on either side, and the defense group may hold left and right defensemen in any mix; a goaltender may be dressed only in goal, and the goaltending group holds only goaltenders. A club short of left defensemen may carry an additional right defenseman, and a club short of centers an additional wing; only in goal does the declared position bind. A club's Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager are players on its active roster and are counted within the published composition in their own position groups; each holds his roster spot for as long as he holds his seat (Rule 2.6). The active roster must at all times hold enough eligible players to ice a full six-on-six lineup, including a goaltender. A club that falls below that minimum through departures or suspensions shall notify the league office immediately; the vacancy is filled by automatic placement under Rule 2.8 while placement remains open, and by the league office thereafter.","Every active-roster player is assigned a position — center, left wing, right wing, left defense, right defense or goaltender — which governs where he may be dressed under the preceding paragraph. The sole exception is a training-camp player, who may be dressed at any position for as long as he is carried in camp (Rule 5.2).","A club may carry any number of training-camp players in addition to its active roster; the commissioner may publish a camp limit as a season setting, and where none is published camp is unlimited. A training-camp player's salary counts against the club's salary cap exactly as an active player's does (Rule 2.5): camp is carried outside the active roster, not outside the payroll. A training-camp player may be dressed at any position and in no more than three (3) games in a game-week (Rule 5.2). Club management may move a player between the active roster and training camp at any time and without limit, in either direction, provided the club continues to satisfy the published composition; the movement deadline (Rule 2.4) does not restrict these moves, which are internal to the club. Team HQ enforces the composition, the position assignments and any camp limit, and refuses any move that would breach them. A player acquired by trade joins his new club on the active roster.","A depth placement is the one exception to the published composition. A player placed on a club by the league office under Rule 2.8 — a player undrafted, or registered after the cutoff — is carried on the active roster as depth: he holds a contract for the season, may be dressed, traded or waived as any other player, and counts against neither the published composition nor any roster limit, so that every registered player has a club."],"full":["Each club ices from an active roster of seventeen (17) players shaped by position group: nine (9) forwards — centers, left wings and right wings in any mix, since a forward may be dressed at center or either wing — six (6) defensemen — left or right in any mix, since a defenseman may be dressed on either side — and two (2) goaltenders, the one position locked to its exact role. A club short of left defensemen may carry an extra right defenseman, and a club short of centers an extra wing; only in goal does the listed position bind. The active roster must at all times hold enough eligible players to ice a full 6v6 lineup, including a goaltender. A club that falls below that minimum through departures or suspensions must notify the league office immediately; the vacancy is filled by automatic placement under Rule 2.2 while placement remains open, and by the league office after that.","Every active-roster player is assigned a position — center, left wing, right wing, left defense, right defense, or goaltender — and the assignment governs where he can dress: an active player may be dressed at any position within his group — a forward at center or either wing, a defenseman at either left or right defense — but a skater is never dressed in goal, nor a goaltender out of it. There are two exceptions: a training-camp player may be dressed at any position for as long as he is carried in camp, and in pre-season games only the club’s Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position (Rule 5.2).","A club may carry up to three (3) training-camp players beyond its seventeen active spots. Their salaries count against the club's salary cap exactly as an active player's does (Rule 2.5) — camp is carried beyond the active roster, not beyond the payroll. A training-camp player may be dressed at any position and, in the regular season and the playoffs, in no more than three (3) games in a game week — the cap does not apply in the pre-season (Rule 5.2). Management may move a player between the active roster and training camp freely — as often as it likes, in either direction, at any point in the season — provided the club still satisfies the active-roster shape above — nine forwards, six defensemen, two goaltenders — and carries no more than three players in camp. There is no limit on how many times a player may change squads, and the movement deadline (Rule 2.4) does not restrict these moves, which are internal to the club. Team HQ enforces the roster shape, the position assignments, and the camp limit, and refuses any move that would breach them. A player acquired by trade joins his new club on the active roster. Pre-season loans are the one exception to the roster limit: a randomly assigned pre-season player rides the active roster as a loan, counts against neither the nine-six-two shape nor any roster cap, and returns to the draft pool when the pre-season ends — a club may hold as many pre-season loans as it is assigned, so every registered player has a club for the pre-season (Rule 0.4)."]},{"id":"2.2","title":"Free agency and signings","paragraphs":["The basic format has no free-agency period and no open market for player contracts. Every registered player enters the draft (Rule 2.8); every player remaining after the draft, and every player who registers after the cutoff, is placed on a club by the league office as depth at the league minimum salary (Rule 2.8); and every contract expires with its season, returning every player to the following season's draft pool (Rule 2.5). No club shall offer a contract to an unrostered player, and no player shall negotiate one.","Contact with a rostered player of another club for the purpose of inducing his departure — tampering — is prohibited and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7.","The one signing available to a club is of a player who has been waived (Rule 2.5). From the conclusion of the draft until the movement deadline (Rule 2.4), any club with room in the player's position group may sign a waived player at the league minimum salary on a contract running to the end of the season. The signing takes effect when it is made, places the player on the club's official roster immediately, and is posted to the transaction log automatically; the league office confirms nothing and has no part in the move. A player shall not dress for a club before he has been signed by it, and any game in which an unsigned player dresses is subject to forfeiture under Chapter 3.","Automatic placement does not apply to a suspended or banned player, who is placed nowhere until reinstated. The basic format recognizes no classes of player rights: no club holds a player's rights once his season ends, and no player is restricted. Should the league designate the full format for a later season, the rights provisions of Appendix A apply from that season, and the league office shall publish, before that season's registration opens, the manner in which a returning player enters it."],"full":["Unrostered registered players are held in the league free agent pool. A club signs a free agent by offering him terms; the signing takes effect the moment the player accepts, and he joins the club’s official roster there and then. The league office confirms nothing and has no say in the move. A player may not dress for a club before he has accepted its offer, and any game in which an unsigned player dresses is subject to forfeiture under Chapter 3.","Contact with rostered players of other clubs for the purpose of inducing a departure — tampering — is prohibited and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7.","A registered player who is neither drafted nor signed is held in the free agent pool, and no registered member is left without a place to play while placement is open. The league office automatically places an unrostered registered player — including anyone who registers after the draft-eligibility deadline or joins mid-season — onto a club that sits below a full active roster, at the league minimum salary. Automatic placement is a league-office action to fill a vacancy, not a club-initiated signing — but it shares the movement deadline’s cutoff: random placement of new players closes at the deadline (Rule 2.4), and a player who registers after it waits for the next season’s draft pool.","Automatic placement never applies to a player under contract: a contracted player who registers is returned to the club that holds the deal (Rule 2.5), never routed to the free agent pool, the draft, or a random assignment. Nor does it apply to a suspended or banned player, who is not placed anywhere until reinstated.","Available players fall into two classes of rights, on the model the NHL uses. A player who has never completed a season on a club's roster is an UNRESTRICTED free agent: he is free to sign with any club that wants him. Once a player has served a club through a season, his rights stay with that club when his contract ends, and he is a RESTRICTED free agent until he has accrued four (4) off-seasons of service, counted one per season completed on a roster. How a former club exercises those rights when another club makes him an offer is set by the league office and published before the first restricted class appears, after Season 1. From the fourth accrued off-season onward he is unrestricted for good. Because the league has no history in its first season, every registered player begins unrestricted; the first restricted free agents appear in the off-season that follows a player's first year on a roster. The number of off-seasons is a league-office setting, and the league publishes it before each signing window opens. A player's current class is shown on his profile and carried by his Discord role. Free agency is for players whose contracts have ended. It is not for rookies: an undrafted first-year is placed on a club by the league office ten minutes after the draft (Rule 2.8) and is never offered a contract on the open market. A player whose contract is in its final season may negotiate only with the club that holds it — either side may open the conversation — at any point in that season, from the day its free agency opens until the free-agency period that FOLLOWS his contract's final season opens — approaching, or being approached by, any other club in that window is tampering under the paragraph above — and may negotiate with any club once that free agency has opened. The club's exclusive right survives the contract's expiry at the season rollover and lapses only when that free agency opens. It neither adds to nor takes from the club's rights over him as a restricted free agent. How a returning player whose deal has ended enters the next season — through that free agency, or through the entry draft — is set by the league office and published before that season's registration opens; the exclusive window and restricted-free-agent rights described here apply from the end of Season 1 onward.","A player whose contract has ended enters open free agency. Clubs send that player contract offers at a salary and term of their choosing, within the cap and contract limits of Rule 2.5. The player may accept, decline, or counter with his own number, and the club may then accept his counter, revise again, or walk away. A signing takes effect the instant the player accepts — he is on the roster immediately, accepting one offer withdraws every other offer he holds, and the move is posted to the transaction log automatically. Players are expected in good faith to take the strongest offer on the table. The league does not forbid a player from signing a lower offer — to play with friends, for instance — but it trusts members not to abuse that latitude to defeat a fair market."]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Trades","paragraphs":["Clubs may trade rostered players by mutual agreement of both clubs' management. Draft picks are not tradeable assets in the basic format: a trade consists of players for players, and an offer that includes a draft pick is refused at the point of entry. A trade takes effect when the receiving club accepts it in the Trade Hub; it is posted to the official transaction log automatically, and a player acquired by trade is eligible for his new club from that club's next scheduled game.","The league office may void any trade it determines was made in bad faith, including a trade intended to circumvent roster limits, to manipulate playoff seeding, or to hold players on behalf of another club.","The league office takes no part in a trade as it is made: the agreement of the two clubs suffices, and the salary-cap and roster checks are applied automatically at acceptance. The transactions department may thereafter review any completed trade and reverse it where, in the department's judgment, it is not a bona fide hockey transaction — a lopsided transfer between allied clubs, or a transaction made to defeat the cap. A reversal returns every player to the club that held him, is posted to the transaction log, and is communicated to both clubs with the department's reasons. A trade may not be reversed once a player included in it has been moved again; the department acts promptly or not at all."],"full":["Clubs may trade rostered players and draft picks by mutual agreement of both clubs’ management. A trade takes effect the moment the second club accepts it in the Trade Hub; it is posted to the official transaction log automatically, and players acquired by trade are eligible for their new club beginning with that club’s next scheduled game.","The league office may void any trade it determines was made in bad faith, including trades intended to circumvent roster limits, manipulate playoff seeding, or park players on behalf of another club.","The league has no say in a trade as it is made: two clubs agreeing is enough, and the salary-cap and roster checks run automatically as the trade is accepted. Afterwards the transactions department may review any completed trade and send it back where, in its judgment, it is not a natural hockey deal — a lopsided giveaway between allied clubs, or a move made to defeat the cap. Reversing a trade returns every player and pick to the club that held it, is posted to the league transaction log, and both clubs are told the reason in the department's own words. A trade cannot be reversed once a player or pick in it has moved on again; the department acts promptly or not at all."]},{"id":"2.4","title":"Movement deadline","paragraphs":["All player movement — the signing of waived players, trades and releases — closes at midnight Eastern Time at the end of the Friday of the fourth (4th) game-week, being the league-posted deadline week announced before the season. After the deadline, rosters are frozen through the end of the playoffs, except that the league office may authorize an emergency goaltender signing for a club unable to ice a goaltender through no fault of its own.","The movement deadline restricts club-initiated signings, trades and releases, and it is also the point at which automatic placement closes: the league office places no newly registered player after the deadline, so that late-season rosters reflect only what clubs did before it. Movement between a club's own active roster and its training camp is internal to the club — no player changes hands — and is not restricted by the deadline (Rule 2.1). Registration for the following season opens when the deadline passes (Rule 1.1)."],"full":["All player movement — signings, trades, and releases — closes at midnight at the end of the Friday of the league-posted deadline week, which is announced before the season. After the deadline, rosters are frozen through the end of the playoffs, except that the league office may authorize an emergency goaltender signing for a club unable to ice a goaltender through no fault of its own, and except a contract extension under Rule 2.5, which changes no roster this season.","The movement deadline restricts club-initiated signings, trades, and releases — and it is also the moment automatic placement closes: the league office does not randomly place newly registered players after the deadline, so late-season rosters are shaped only by what clubs did before it. A contracted player who registers is returned to his own club at any time under Rule 2.5; that is a contract right, not a placement, and no deadline touches it. Movement between a club's own active roster and its training camp is internal to the club — no player changes hands — and the deadline does not restrict it (Rule 2.1)."]},{"id":"2.5","title":"Salary cap and contracts","paragraphs":["Every club operates under a hard salary cap, set by the commissioner for each season and published on every club's cap sheet — {{CAP}} for the current season. A club shall not ice a lineup, complete a trade or claim a waived player if the move would place its payroll above the cap. A training-camp player's salary forms part of that payroll: camp is carried outside the active roster but not outside the cap. A club that carries a player in camp bears his full salary against the cap from the moment he is placed there, whether or not he dresses, and recalling him to the active roster changes nothing about his cost. A depth placement's salary counts in the same manner. Compliance is verified automatically at every roster move, and the league office may void any transaction that breaches the cap.","Every player holds a contract with a cap hit and a term of one (1) season — the season in which it is signed. A drafted player's contract carries the salary fixed for his round by Rule 2.8; a depth placement's contract, and a waived player's on re-signing, carries the league minimum salary. The league minimum salary is $750,000, and every salary in the league is a multiple of $250,000 above it — $750,000, $1,000,000, $1,250,000 and so on. That increment governs every salary in the league: the draft scale of Rule 2.8 is fixed on it, and the fixed management values of Rule 2.6 lie on the same lattice. A figure off the lattice is refused where it is entered rather than rounded.","The basic format provides for no contract extension, no re-signing and no retained player rights. Every contract expires when its season ends, the player returns to the pool, and he enters the following season's draft on the same footing as every other registered player (Rule 2.8). A club may not offer a player terms for a future season, and nothing a club does in a season confers any claim on a player for the next. The cap year is the season.","Waiving a player removes his cap hit from the club immediately and opens a twenty-four (24) hour waiver period. A claiming club assumes the player at his pre-waiver salary, reinstated as it stood; a player who clears waivers unclaimed may be signed by any club with room in his position group, at the league minimum salary, until the movement deadline (Rule 2.2). A player returning from a league suspension is reinstated at his pre-suspension salary.","Cap space, contract values and trade-block status are confidential club information, visible only to the club's management group and the league office, and are subject to the confidentiality standard of Rule 2.3.","A contract does not substitute for registration. Every player must register for every season; registration is what enters a player in the draft pool (Rule 2.8). Because no contract outlives its season, a player who does not register sits the season out; nothing is owed to him and nothing is charged against any club's cap."],"full":["Every club operates under a hard salary cap, set by the commissioner for each season and published on every club’s cap sheet — {{CAP}} for the current season. A club may not ice a lineup, complete a trade, or claim a waived player if the move would push its active payroll above the cap. A training-camp player's salary is part of that payroll: camp sits outside the seventeen active spots, but not outside the cap. A club that carries a player in camp pays his full salary against the cap from the moment he is placed there, whether or not he ever dresses, and calling him up to the active roster changes nothing about what he costs. Compliance is checked automatically at every roster move, and the league office may void any transaction that breaches it.","Every player carries a contract with a cap hit and a term of one to three seasons; a drafted player's first contract, and a contract written by automatic placement, runs exactly one season at the salary Rule 2.8 fixes; a new deal may follow it only through the extension window below or through free agency. The league minimum salary is $750,000, and every salary in the league is a multiple of $250,000 — the minimum, then $1,000,000, $1,250,000, $1,500,000 and so on upward. That single step governs every salary in the league, whether it is negotiated or set by rule: open free agency under Rule 2.2, every counter and revision inside a negotiation, and the draft scale of Rule 2.8, which is fixed rather than agreed. The fixed management values of Rule 2.6 sit on the same lattice. A figure off that lattice is refused where it is entered rather than quietly rounded, so the number a club offers is always the number the player is asked to accept. A contract that reaches the end of its term returns the player to free agency under Rule 2.2 unless he has signed an extension (below) — and until that free agency opens, the club that held the deal alone may re-sign him.","The cap year runs from one free-agency opening to the next. A contract's money counts only against the seasons it covers; when its final season ends the deal is closed at the season rollover, but the club's exclusive right to re-sign the player survives until the free agency that follows that season opens — until then he is neither a free agent nor anyone else's to approach. A club re-signs its own player through an extension: at any point in the final season of his deal — from the day that season's free agency opens — the club may offer him a new contract from Team HQ, or he may open with his own number from his dashboard, and the two negotiate exactly as in free agency — offer, counter, revise, accept or walk away — at a salary on the $250,000 lattice and a term of one to three seasons. An extension accepted during the final season is a signed deal for the following season, and comes into force with that season's cap year: nothing about the current season changes, and it is checked against the following season's cap — counting every deal already signed for that season and the three front-office seats at their fixed values — rather than this season's, because the space a club will have then is what it will have once its expiring deals have turned over. The site refuses any signing, now or for a future season, that would not fit inside the cap it is checked against; there is no exception. A player re-signed after the rollover, before that season's free agency opens, is signed for the season now under way: the deal takes effect the moment he accepts, is checked against that season's cap, and seats him on the club once he is registered. A signed extension travels with the player if he is traded and is voided if he is waived. A player may hold one signed extension at a time, and while he holds one no club may offer him another. No club other than the one that holds his current deal may offer him anything until that deal has ended and free agency has opened (Rule 2.2). Team HQ shows every club its committed money and cap space for the current season and the three that follow, from the same arithmetic the cap checks use, so a club plans against exactly what will be enforced.","Waiving a player clears his cap hit immediately and opens a 24-hour waiver window. A claiming club takes on the player at his pre-waiver salary, reinstated exactly as it stood; a player who clears unclaimed becomes a free agent. A player returning from a league suspension is reinstated at his pre-suspension salary and remaining term.","Cap space, contract values, and trade-block status are confidential team information. They are visible only to a club's management group and the league office, and are covered by the confidentiality standard in Rule 2.3.","A contract does not replace registration. Every player must register for every season — including every season of a multi-year deal. Registering while under contract returns the player directly to the club that holds the deal, at the contracted salary: no draft, no free agency, no random assignment. The sign-up requirement in Chapter 0 applies to everyone, veteran and rookie, signed and unsigned alike.","If a player under contract fails to register and the club's ownership is unchanged, the deal becomes dead money: the full salary continues to count against the club's salary cap, and the player cannot play — no roster spot exists, and the player may not be dressed, drafted, signed, or placed anywhere. The hold lasts until the player registers, which reactivates the deal at no net cap change, or until the contract's term ends.","If the club changes hands during the off-season — a different owner takes the seat between one season and the next — and the contracted player is still unregistered past the sign-up deadline, the contract is voided: the salary comes off the books entirely, and the player is suspended through what would have been the deal's final season. A club that changes owners is never saddled with an unsigned player's salary, and a player who walks away sits out exactly the seasons the contract covered. (A club that simply sits without an owner for a stretch has not changed hands — its unsigned deals stay as dead money until the player registers, nothing is voided.) These suspensions are recorded in the league's discipline log and lift automatically when their final season completes."]},{"id":"2.6","title":"Team management structure","paragraphs":["Each club is led by an Owner, who is appointed by the league office through the application process, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager. The front-office seats are the Owner’s alone: the Owner nominates the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager — a nomination goes to the league office’s reviewers, who approve or deny it — and the Owner may remove a sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager at any time, without a league-office step; the removal takes effect at once, the seat falls vacant, and the league office is told. A General Manager or Assistant General Manager nominates and removes nobody. Each seat is held by one person and one person only, and one person holds at most one club seat league-wide. An appointment through the application process into a seat that is already held is refused: the sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager steps down, or is removed by the Owner or the league office, before a successor can be approved, so no manager is displaced by an appointment without being told. The league office retains its power to seat and unseat management directly under this chapter, including removal under the standards set out below. These roles form the club’s management group and are the only accounts with access to the club’s Team HQ and its confidential team tools — the availability grid, the lineup builder, the roster cap sheet, the free-agent board, the draft board and the Trade Hub. Every member of the management group — Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager alike — has the same Team HQ, subject only to the access the Owner sets under the next paragraph. Every roster move that touches the club — a signing, waiver or release, each step of a trade, a contract offer and its answer, a pre-season loan, a squad move or a trade-block flag — is delivered to the whole management group as a site notification and as a post in the club’s private Discord room the moment it is recorded, alongside the public transaction log; a placement made in bulk by the league office arrives as one summary naming the players. Every club must hold all three seats before the entry draft begins (Rule 2.8).","The Owner sets what each manager may do, from Team HQ → Management, for every front-office page — the roster, the lineup builder, the schedule’s server picks, the game stats desk, the Trade Hub, the free-agent board and the draft. For each page the General Manager’s access and the Assistant General Manager’s access is one of three things: full, in which case the manager acts freely; subject to the Owner’s approval, in which case the manager sees the page but every move made on it — a waiver, a contract or extension offer, a trade proposed, accepted or declined, a lineup or line, a server pick, a draft pick or a draft-board change — is queued and takes effect only when the Owner approves it; or withheld, in which case the page is hidden from that seat and its moves are refused. The default for every seat and page is full access, with one exception: the Management page — the front office, this permissions table and the approval queue — is the Owner’s alone and is withheld from the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager unless the Owner opens it to them. A queued move runs through the league’s ordinary checks at the moment it is approved, and a move that can no longer be made by then — a lineup past its lock, an offer already withdrawn, a pick already used — fails rather than half-applies, and both the Owner and the manager are told why. What the Owner reads and approves is a description the league builds from the move itself — the player, the terms, the clubs, the lineup — never text the manager wrote, so what is approved is what runs. A manager may withdraw a queued move before the Owner decides it, and a newer request for the same thing from the same manager replaces the older one still waiting. The Owner’s own moves never need approval, the Owner cannot limit the Owner’s own seat, and none of this limits the league office. The game stats desk and this Management page carry no roster or cap moves, so they are either open to a seat or withheld — there is nothing on them to approve.","Management contracts carry fixed cap values: the Owner counts $0 against the cap, and the General Manager and Assistant General Manager (and any additional tertiary management contract) each count a flat $3,000,000. A manager is a player on his club's active roster and, under the basic format, holds one of the club's active-roster spots in his own position group within the published composition (Rule 2.1). A player is protected while he holds a management role: he may not be waived, and he may not be included in a trade, until the role is vacated.","Owner and staff applications with no open seat may be placed on a waitlist by the league office. When a seat opens — an owner resigns or is not renewed, or a staff member steps down — the longest-waiting eligible application fills it automatically, in the order applications were waitlisted; eligibility is re-checked at appointment against Rule 2.7, so a waitlisted applicant who has since taken a conflicting seat is passed over, not appointed. The appointee and the commissioners are notified the moment it happens, and the office may reverse any automatic appointment on review.","Managers are held to the activity and on-ice standards the commissioner sets each season — minimum weekly availability and, where applicable, baseline performance thresholds by position. A manager who falls short of those standards may be reviewed and, if warranted, removed and replaced under this chapter.","Trade discussions, offers, and block listings are restricted to the management groups involved and the league office. Carrying that information outside the management group — to players, to rival clubs, or to the public — is a conduct violation under Rule 1.2 and Rule 2.3."],"full":["Each club is led by an Owner, who is appointed by the league office through the application process, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager. The front-office seats are the Owner’s alone: the Owner nominates the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager — a nomination goes to the league office’s reviewers, who approve or deny it — and the Owner may remove a sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager at any time, without a league-office step; the removal takes effect at once, the seat falls vacant, and the league office is told. A General Manager or Assistant General Manager nominates and removes nobody. Each seat is held by one person and one person only, and one person holds at most one club seat league-wide. An appointment through the application process into a seat that is already held is refused: the sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager steps down, or is removed by the Owner or the league office, before a successor can be approved, so no manager is displaced by an appointment without being told. The league office retains its power to seat and unseat management directly under this chapter, including removal under the standards set out below. These roles form the club’s management group and are the only accounts with access to the club’s Team HQ and its confidential team tools — the availability grid, the lineup builder, the roster cap sheet, the free-agent board, the draft board and the Trade Hub. Every member of the management group — Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager alike — has the same Team HQ, subject only to the access the Owner sets under the next paragraph. Every roster move that touches the club — a signing, waiver or release, each step of a trade, a contract offer and its answer, a pre-season loan, a squad move or a trade-block flag — is delivered to the whole management group as a site notification and as a post in the club’s private Discord room the moment it is recorded, alongside the public transaction log; a placement made in bulk by the league office arrives as one summary naming the players. Every club must hold all three seats before the entry draft begins (Rule 2.8).","The Owner sets what each manager may do, from Team HQ → Management, for every front-office page — the roster, the lineup builder, the schedule’s server picks, the game stats desk, the Trade Hub, the free-agent board and the draft. For each page the General Manager’s access and the Assistant General Manager’s access is one of three things: full, in which case the manager acts freely; subject to the Owner’s approval, in which case the manager sees the page but every move made on it — a waiver, a contract or extension offer, a trade proposed, accepted or declined, a lineup or line, a server pick, a draft pick or a draft-board change — is queued and takes effect only when the Owner approves it; or withheld, in which case the page is hidden from that seat and its moves are refused. The default for every seat and page is full access, with one exception: the Management page — the front office, this permissions table and the approval queue — is the Owner’s alone and is withheld from the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager unless the Owner opens it to them. A queued move runs through the league’s ordinary checks at the moment it is approved, and a move that can no longer be made by then — a lineup past its lock, an offer already withdrawn, a pick already used — fails rather than half-applies, and both the Owner and the manager are told why. What the Owner reads and approves is a description the league builds from the move itself — the player, the terms, the clubs, the lineup — never text the manager wrote, so what is approved is what runs. A manager may withdraw a queued move before the Owner decides it, and a newer request for the same thing from the same manager replaces the older one still waiting. The Owner’s own moves never need approval, the Owner cannot limit the Owner’s own seat, and none of this limits the league office. The game stats desk and this Management page carry no roster or cap moves, so they are either open to a seat or withheld — there is nothing on them to approve.","Management contracts carry fixed cap values: the Owner counts $0 against the cap, and the General Manager and Assistant General Manager (and any additional tertiary management contract) each count a flat $3,000,000. A player is protected while he holds a management role — he may not be waived, and he may not be included in a trade, until the role is vacated.","Owner and staff applications with no open seat may be placed on a waitlist by the league office. When a seat opens — an owner resigns or is not renewed, or a staff member steps down — the longest-waiting eligible application fills it automatically, in the order applications were waitlisted; eligibility is re-checked at appointment against Rule 2.7, so a waitlisted applicant who has since taken a conflicting seat is passed over, not appointed. The appointee and the commissioners are notified the moment it happens, and the office may reverse any automatic appointment on review.","Managers are held to the activity and on-ice standards the commissioner sets each season — minimum weekly availability and, where applicable, baseline performance thresholds by position. A manager who falls short of those standards may be reviewed and, if warranted, removed and replaced under this chapter.","Trade discussions, offers, and block listings are restricted to the management groups involved and the league office. Carrying that information outside the management group — to players, to rival clubs, or to the public — is a conduct violation under Rule 1.2 and Rule 2.3."]},{"id":"2.7","title":"League staff and management separation","paragraphs":["The league office and a club's front office may not be held at once, with the single exception set out below. A commissioner or staff member may not own or manage a club — no Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager role — and, the other way round, anyone holding a club's Owner, GM, or AGM seat may not sit on the staff. Within the office, a commissioner may serve as staff: commissioners and staff work disputes, discipline, and applications together. What the separation forbids is holding a league-office seat and a club's front-office seat at the same time, so decisions about teams and staff are made without a stake in the outcome.","Media is a department apart, and an exclusive one. A staff member on Media holds Media and no other department, and holds no ballot: not on a staff vote of any kind, not on a rule change, and not on a season award. The department reports on the league rather than deciding for it. In exchange, Media is the one department that may also hold a club's Owner, GM, or Assistant GM seat — and the two halves of that bargain are what make each other safe, because a staff member who decides nothing cannot decide anything in favor of the club they run. A staff member who wants a second department, or a vote, gives up both the Media seat and, if they hold one, the club seat. None of this binds a commissioner, whose office carries every department and every vote by definition.","Staff and commissioners may still play. Holding an office does not bar a member from being a rostered player on a club; it bars only the management roles that would put them on both sides of a league decision.","A former staff member must sit out one full season before taking a management role, and a former commissioner must sit out two, so that the authority of the office does not follow a member straight onto a club's bench. The league office may grant a narrow exception only where a club would otherwise fold, and only as a documented last resort."]},{"id":"2.8","title":"The entry draft","paragraphs":["Rosters are rebuilt each season through an entry draft held on the Saturday following the sign-up cutoff (Chapter 0). The commissioner shall determine the number of rounds for each season's draft and shall publish it to the clubs, together with the roster composition of Rule 2.1, before the draft; the number published for a season is binding for its duration, and the draft board is built to it. Clubs select in a snake order — the drawn order is followed in the first round, reversed in the second and alternated thereafter — with selections made live on the site against a per-selection clock. The season's calendar around the draft is set out in Chapter 0; this rule states the binding requirements.","No club shall draft without a full front office. Every club must hold an Owner, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6) before the draft may begin. The league office seats any open post first; the draft does not start while a seat is vacant, and the draft room identifies any club still short before the night.","The draft order is drawn at random by the commissioner before the draft and published to the clubs before it begins. Each season's order is drawn afresh and bears no relation to the previous season's standings, playoffs or champion; an expansion club enters the draw on equal terms with every other club. The commissioner may substitute a manual order only where the league office has ruled that one is required.","Every player who registers by the sign-up cutoff — 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding the draft (Chapter 0, Step two) — enters the draft pool. There is no appearance requirement and no exemption: registration by the cutoff is the sole test. A registration filed after the cutoff does not enter the pool; that player is placed on a club as depth under the placement paragraph below, until placement closes at the movement deadline.","A drafted player is paid according to the round of his selection and not his position within it. The final round pays the league minimum salary, and each round above it adds one increment of $250,000 (Rule 2.5): in a draft of N rounds, round N pays $750,000 and round one pays $750,000 plus $250,000 multiplied by (N − 1), so that the scale follows the number of rounds published for the season and the final round always pays the minimum. The full scale for the season is published with the round count, before the draft, and is displayed in the draft room. Every selection in a round carries an identical cap hit — the first overall selection and the last selection of the round cost a club the same — and a selection deferred under the following paragraphs and used later carries the cap hit of its own round. Salary is fixed by this scale at the moment the selection is made, whether the club selects for itself or its clock expires and the league office selects from its board.","Each club shall maintain a private draft board — a ranked list of the players it wishes to select, visible only to that club's management and the league office. If a club's clock expires, the league office selects the highest-ranked available player on that club's board, and only when the board is exhausted does it select the best available eligible player overall. A club is not assigned a player it did not rank while its board holds an available name.","Draft picks are not club assets under the basic format and may not be traded (Rule 2.3). A club uses its own selection in every round or defers it; a deferred selection is not forfeited and remains the club's to use at any time before the draft is concluded.","The commissioner conducts the draft from the Control Center and may pause and resume it, reset the selection clock, defer a selection, reverse a completed selection, make a selection on a club's behalf, or conclude the draft. Concluding the draft releases any unused selections and begins a ten (10) minute period, after which every registered player still without a club is placed on one by the league office, in random order, at the league minimum salary, as depth (Rule 2.1) — first into any vacancy in his position group within the published composition, and otherwise onto the club then carrying the fewest players — on a contract for the season (Rule 2.5). No club selects, and no registered player is left without a club. The same placement seats every late registrant until the movement deadline closes placement (Rule 2.4). The ten-minute period exists so that the league office may still reverse a selection."],"full":["Rosters are rebuilt each season through an entry draft held after the pre-season (Chapter 0). Clubs select in the same order in every round — the order never reverses — over fourteen (14) rounds, with picks made live on the site against a per-pick clock. The overall narrative of the season's calendar around the draft is set out in Chapter 0; this rule states the binding requirements.","No club drafts without a full front office. Every club must hold an Owner, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6) before the draft can begin. The league office seats any open post first; the draft will not start while a seat is empty, and the draft room names any club still short so nobody discovers it on the night.","The draft order is set by the commissioner in one of five ways: by an NHL-style draft lottery drawn from the previous season — clubs that missed the playoffs draw weighted odds for the top two selections, with the weakest club holding the most tickets, the rest of the non-playoff group follows in reverse standings, and playoff clubs pick after them in reverse order of finish, the champion last; by reverse pre-season standings, so the weakest club picks first; by a weighted lottery over the pre-season table, in which every club can win the first pick but the weakest hold the most tickets; by a pure random shuffle; or by a manual order the commissioner arranges. A first season, having no previous season to draw on, uses a pure random shuffle in place of the lottery. A club with no previous-season record of its own — an expansion club — enters the lottery as if it had finished last. Whichever method is used, the drawn order is published to the clubs before the draft begins.","Every player who registers by the draft-eligibility deadline enters the draft pool, provided he meets the pre-season appearance requirement. A player who is randomly assigned for the pre-season, and who is not already carried on a club's full-time roster, must appear in at least three (3) pre-season games to remain draft-eligible. Returning players and club management are exempt from the requirement and may play the pre-season or sit it out as they choose. Club management is obligated to distribute pre-season appearances as widely as it can, so that every player who needs three games has a real opportunity to play them; a club that leaves eligible players short without cause is reviewed by the league office. The deadline is 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft (Chapter 0.3); a registration filed after it is never in the pool, however many pre-season games its owner plays. A player who registers after the deadline is not lost: he is placed on a club automatically under Rule 2.2, up until random placement closes at the movement deadline.","A drafted player is paid by the round in which he is selected rather than by his position inside it. The final round of the draft pays the league minimum, and each round above it adds one $250,000 step (Rule 2.5) — so the scale is set by the number of rounds the commissioner calls that season, and the last round always pays the minimum however many there are. In this season's fourteen-round draft that is round one $4,000,000, round two $3,750,000, round three $3,500,000, round four $3,250,000, round five $3,000,000, round six $2,750,000, round seven $2,500,000, round eight $2,250,000, round nine $2,000,000, round ten $1,750,000, round eleven $1,500,000, round twelve $1,250,000, round thirteen $1,000,000 and round fourteen $750,000, for a full class of $33,250,000 — which, with the three management contracts of Rule 2.6, brings a club that uses every pick to within $750,000 of the cap before free agency opens. Every pick in a round carries an identical cap hit — first overall and tenth overall cost a club the same. A club that trades within a round moves no money; a club that trades across rounds moves one step per round, and a pick that is skipped and used later still costs its own round. Salary is fixed by this scale at the moment the pick is made, whether the club picks for itself or its clock expires and the league picks from its board.","Each club keeps a private draft board — a ranked list of the players it wants, visible only to that club's management and the league office. If a club's clock expires on the night, the league selects the best available player from that club's own board, and only if the board is exhausted does it fall to the best available eligible player overall. A club is never auto-assigned a player it did not rank while its board still holds an available name.","Draft picks are club assets and may be traded under the ordinary trade rules of Rule 2.3, within the cap of Rule 2.5 — before the draft and during it, exactly as on a real draft floor. A traded pick belongs to its new club the moment the trade is accepted, including a pick currently on the clock, whose timer continues unchanged. A pick that has already been used cannot be traded, and an offer containing one is refused as stale. Trades made during the draft are announced on the transaction wire like any other.","The commissioner runs the draft from the Control Center and may pause and resume it, reset the pick clock, skip a pick, reverse a completed pick, make a pick on a club's behalf, or conclude the draft. A skipped pick is not forfeited — it remains the club's to use at any time before the draft ends. Concluding the draft releases any unused picks and begins a ten-minute countdown, after which every registered player still without a club — undrafted, or short of the three-game requirement — is placed on one by the league office at the league minimum, in random order, seated where a club has room in his position group (Rule 2.1). No club chooses. The placement is a one-season contract (Rule 2.5). The ten minutes exist so the office can still reverse a pick; anyone the office cannot seat, because every club with room at his position is at its cap or roster limit, is reported to the commissioners rather than left in silence."]},{"id":"2.9","title":"Position changes","paragraphs":["A member's position is declared at registration and governs his placement: clubs draft against positional need, and the league office places undrafted and late-registering players by declared position. A member who wishes to change his declared position shall file a position-change request with the league office from the league office page. Staff decide the request; no member decides his own. This rule governs the position at which a player is registered; Rule 2.1 governs where a rostered player may be dressed on a given night.","Position changes close at the sign-up cutoff — 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding the draft. After the cutoff a member's declared position is fixed for the season: no request may be filed and none may be approved. A request already pending may be declined and closed so that none remains open. The deadline follows the published calendar rather than a fixed date — if the draft is re-spaced, the deadline moves with it — and the commissioner retains a narrow authority to correct a position after the cutoff where the error was the league office's own.","The cutoff is placed there because the draft order and every club's draft board are prepared against the positions with which the pool is published, and the league office places the undrafted by the same positions; closing at the cutoff ensures that the pool the clubs draft from is the pool they prepared against."],"full":["A member's position is chosen when he registers and governs where he is placed: the pre-season assigns players across clubs by position, and clubs draft and sign against positional need. A member who wants a different position files a position-change request with the league office from the league office page. Staff decide it, and no member decides his own request. This rule governs the position a player is registered AT; Rule 2.1 separately governs where a rostered player may be dressed on a given night.","Position changes close at 11:59 PM Eastern on the Tuesday before the first pre-season game. After that a member's position is set for the season: no request may be filed, and none may be approved. A request already sitting in the queue may still be declined and closed, so nothing is left hanging. The deadline follows the published schedule rather than a fixed calendar date — if the pre-season is re-spaced, the deadline moves with it — and the commissioner retains a narrow power to correct a position after it where the league office itself made the error.","Why it closes there: a position that changes after the pre-season has started invalidates both of the things built on it. Clubs are assigned their pre-season rosters by position, and the draft order and every club's board are prepared against the positions the pool is published with. Closing on the Tuesday leaves the day before the opener for the office to settle the list and for clubs to see the pool they will actually be playing and drafting from."]}]},{"num":3,"title":"Scheduling, forfeits & reschedules","sections":[{"id":"3.1","title":"The official schedule","paragraphs":["The CGHL regular season runs six (6) game-weeks — three nights a week, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, three games a night, for 54 games per club — with matchups and puck-drop times published on the official league schedule. A game-week skipped for an observed holiday does not shorten the season: the schedule steps over that week and runs one week later, so the game count above always holds. The schedule as posted by the league office is binding on all clubs. Puck-drop times are stated in Eastern Time unless otherwise noted, and clubs are responsible for converting to their local time. Game-weeks holding a league-observed holiday are skipped in full; the commissioner sets each season’s observed holidays, and the schedule as generated already reflects them. The playoffs open the game week immediately after the final regular-season week — if an observed holiday pushes the season later, the playoff date moves with it, so the regular season can never run into its own postseason.","Clubs are expected to be lobby-ready at the scheduled puck-drop time. Lobby-ready means a full eligible lineup is online, in the league Discord, and prepared to enter the private game upon code release under Rule 4.2."],"full":["The CGHL regular season runs eight (8) game-weeks — three nights a week, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, three games a night, for 72 games per club — with matchups and puck-drop times published on the official league schedule. A game-week skipped for an observed holiday does not shorten the season: the schedule steps over that week and runs one week later, so the game count above always holds. The schedule as posted by the league office is binding on all clubs. Puck-drop times are stated in Eastern Time unless otherwise noted, and clubs are responsible for converting to their local time. Game-weeks holding a league-observed holiday are skipped in full; the commissioner sets each season’s observed holidays, and the schedule as generated already reflects them. The playoffs open the game week immediately after the final regular-season week — if an observed holiday pushes the season later, the playoff date moves with it, so the regular season can never run into its own postseason.","Clubs are expected to be lobby-ready at the scheduled puck-drop time. Lobby-ready means a full eligible lineup is online, in the league Discord, and prepared to enter the private game upon code release under Rule 4.2."]},{"id":"3.2","title":"Late starts, the grace period and forfeits","paragraphs":["Games begin with a search at the scheduled puck-drop time. Lateness is measured from that time and is answered with penalties served at the start of the game once it begins: at five (5) minutes past game time the late club takes one (1) penalty; at eight (8) minutes past, two (2) penalties in total. Where two penalties are owed, the club that was ready chooses how they are served — both at once, for a five-on-three power play, or one after the other, for two consecutive five-on-four power plays.","At ten (10) minutes past game time the game is a forfeit, recorded as a 1-0 win for the club that was ready. No individual player statistics are applied to any player from a forfeited game, so that no player's record carries statistics from a game that was not played. The game still counts toward each player's weekly appearance total under Rule 5.2, and that cannot be undone to restore availability. A club may concede a forfeit before puck drop if it knows it cannot field six.","A club that is late because of matchmaking trouble in its immediately preceding game is not penalized for that lateness. The exemption is lost where the club shows a clear lack of urgency in getting the next game started. The league office rules on any dispute, and repeated reliance on this exemption is reviewed under Chapter 7.","The ten-minute forfeit stands unless it is waived by a member of league staff who is not playing in, managing, or otherwise involved in the game in question. The two clubs may not waive it between themselves. The penalties owed for lateness are a different matter: the club that was ready may waive them, in whole or in part, if both clubs agree — that is a decision for the two clubs, made between them before the game starts, and the league office records the outcome rather than second-guessing it.","The league does not record double forfeits. If neither club fields a lineup and no forfeit is claimed, the game has no result — the commissioner voids it, and a voided game is kept out of the standings. A one-sided failure is a forfeit, not a reschedule; a reschedule (Rule 3.3) is available only when both clubs have a genuine connection problem. Disputes over a claimed forfeit go to the league office, which may order a replay when neither side has stream evidence.","A club that knows in advance it cannot play should pursue a reschedule under Rule 3.3 rather than allow a forfeit. Repeated forfeits are referred to the league office for review under Chapter 7."]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Reschedules","paragraphs":["A game may be rescheduled only by written agreement of both clubs’ management, filed with and approved by the league office before the originally scheduled puck drop. The league office will not approve reschedules that push a game past the end of the week in which it was originally scheduled unless exceptional circumstances exist.","Where the clubs cannot agree, the game stands as scheduled. The league office may on its own authority move a game for league-wide technical outages, EA server failures, or other events outside the clubs' control."]}]},{"num":4,"title":"Game settings & servers","sections":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Official game configuration","paragraphs":["All CGHL games are played in EA Sports NHL 27 as 6v6 club games using the league-published settings sheet, which forms part of this rulebook by reference. Games played on any other settings are unofficial and will be replayed on correct settings at the league office's direction.","Regulation is three (3) periods of four (4) minutes each. A game tied at the end of regulation goes to overtime played five-on-five at full strength, in periods of the same four-minute length, sudden death — the first goal wins. Overtime periods are played back to back, continuously, until a team scores; CGHL does not use a shootout in any game, and no CGHL game can end in a tie.","The overtime format is the same in the regular season and the playoffs. A game decided in overtime is a win for the club that scores and an overtime loss for the club that does not; the points those results carry are set out in Rule 8.1.","The in-game clock displays each four-minute period as twenty (20) minutes, and the final minute of the third period runs in real time rather than at the accelerated rate. In the private lobby, each club selects the side shown on the league schedule — the club listed as Home selects Home, and the club listed as Away selects Away — so the recorded box score always matches the fixture."]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Private game codes","paragraphs":["League games are played in private lobbies secured by a game code issued by the league office. Codes are released to the two competing clubs — their management and the players on that night’s lineup — no earlier than thirty (30) minutes before the scheduled puck drop.","Game codes are confidential. A code may be shared only with rostered players dressing in that game and may never be posted publicly — including in open Discord channels, streams, social media, or any other public venue. Publicly sharing a game code, or admitting a non-rostered player to a league lobby, is a conduct violation subject to discipline under Chapter 7, and the league office may order the game replayed on a fresh code."]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Disconnections","paragraphs":["If a player loses connection during play, that player's club leaves the game at the next whistle. Every statistic recorded between the disconnection and that whistle counts in full — for both clubs and for every player, whoever they are. Nothing earned in that window is struck from the record.","The clubs then reload and replay the game, and the club that lost the player takes one (1) penalty within one (1) minute of the game-clock time at which the disconnection occurred. The replayed game is thereby brought back into balance with the abandoned one.","However many sessions a game takes — the abandoned sitting and every reload — they are combined into a single game record through the league's stats import, so that the box score reads as one complete game and no player's statistics are divided across fragments.","A second disconnection by the same club in the same game is answered with two (2) penalties on the next reload. The club that did not disconnect chooses how they are served — both at once, for a five-on-three power play, or one after the other, for two consecutive five-on-four power plays.","League games are played on four-minute periods, which the in-game clock displays as twenty (20) minutes, and the final minute of the third period runs in real time rather than at the accelerated rate. A third-period disconnection therefore costs more real time than the game clock suggests. Where the disconnection occurs in the third period, the owed penalty is taken five (5) minutes of game clock earlier than the time of the disconnection, which accounts for that difference.","Where the disconnection occurs within the first five (5) minutes of the third period, the clubs do not replay a full game. They reload and play the entire first period of the new game as if it were the third period, and the club that disconnected takes its owed penalty or penalties as soon as possible after puck drop. Playing a whole period this way accounts for the additional real time carried by the final minute of a third period.","A third disconnection by the same club in the same game ends the game as a forfeit loss for that club. All statistics are retained: the sessions played are combined into a single game through the league's stats import, and the score recorded is the combined score standing at the third disconnection. The game is published as a forfeit loss — marked FFL on the result — and that ruling stands even where the forfeiting club finished with more goals; the goals themselves, and every individual line, are kept exactly as they were earned. This is the one forfeit that keeps its numbers: the game was played, and the players who played it keep what they did.","If the lobby itself fails — a full crash or an EA server outage affecting both clubs — captains must capture evidence of the score and game clock at failure. The league office will order the game resumed from the recorded score and time where feasible, and replayed in full where it is not. A club may not use a disconnect, real or feigned, to gain a stoppage, delay, or matchup advantage.","Late starts and disconnections are recorded against the club by the league office in the game incident log, which is how the second and third occurrences in this rule are counted."]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Connection standards","paragraphs":["Players are expected to compete on a stable connection. Persistent, game-affecting lag attributable to a single player may be reviewed by the league office, which may require the player to sit until the issue is resolved. Deliberate manipulation of one's connection to induce lag is cheating and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7."]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Positional builds and X-Factor restrictions","paragraphs":["CGHL is decided by skill, not by loadout. Every player dresses on a legal build for his position and keeps his X-Factors within the restrictions of this rule, which the league maintains so that games turn on how a player plays rather than on a chosen ability. This rule binds every game — regular season and playoffs. A player who loads into a game with a banned ability, a banned build, a pre-built loadout or a special-character cosmetic does not play that game as loaded: the game is restarted, and the offending player serves one (1) minor penalty at the start of the restarted game, without prejudice to further discipline under Chapter 7.","The following X-Factor abilities are banned at every tier the game offers them: Truculence, Spark Plug, Hipster, Pressure+, Unstoppable Force and Big Rig. Two further abilities are banned at the Red tier only — Stick ’Em Up and Big Tipper — and may be equipped below it. The banned-ability and banned-build lists in this rule are written against NHL 27 and apply as written; the office keeps the definitive list with the settings sheet and may add to it at any time as the game is patched or the meta shifts, and any change is posted with that week’s schedule and in the changelog.","The league is played with standard created players only, built by hand. Every pre-built loadout the game offers is banned: no player shall dress on a ready-made build, whatever its name or position. Special-character player classes — the pre-built gimmick classes such as Moonlight, Hammer, Bones, Wally, Deepfreeze, Surge, and Vector — are banned, as is every special or mascot character, and every special-character cosmetic is banned with them: no item, skin or effect belonging to a special character may be worn on a created player. These names are illustrative, not exhaustive: no pre-built loadout, no special class, no special character and none of its cosmetics may be dressed in any CGHL game, at any position.","Builds are locked to their position group, and no enforcer build may be used at either position. A forward may not use the Enforcer (ENF) build, nor any defenseman build — Defensive Defenseman (DD), Offensive Defenseman (OD), Enforcer Defenseman (ENFD, tagged EFD in some menus), Two-Way Defenseman (TWD), or Puck-Moving Defenseman (PMD). A defenseman may not use the Enforcer Defenseman (ENFD) build, nor any forward build — Grinder (GRN), Playmaker (PLY), Sniper (SNP), Power Forward (PWF), Two-Way Forward (TWF), Enforcer (ENF), or Dangler (DNG). What remains is the legal set: a forward builds as a Sniper, Playmaker, Grinder, Power Forward, Two-Way Forward, or Dangler; a defenseman builds as a Defensive, Offensive, Two-Way, or Puck-Moving Defenseman. No build outside this legal set may be dressed at that position.","Responsibility for fielding legal builds rests with the player and his club’s management. Managers should confirm every dressed player’s build and abilities before lineups lock under Rule 5.3. When a banned ability, build, loadout or cosmetic is found after a game has loaded, either club may call for the restart, and the offending club shall restart; the penalty of this rule is served by the offending player as a minor penalty taken at the start of the restarted game and verified by the officials against the box score. A club that declines to restart forfeits the game under Rule 3.2. The league office maintains the banned-ability list with the settings sheet (Rule 4.1) and announces any change to it with that week’s schedule."]}]},{"num":5,"title":"Availability & lineups","sections":[{"id":"5.1","title":"Weekly availability","paragraphs":["Every rostered player must submit weekly availability for the upcoming game week through the league’s availability system — an answer of Available or Not Available for each of the club’s games that week, with an optional note per night — no later than 7:30 PM Eastern Time on the day the game week’s first games are played — Wednesday in a normal week, 90 minutes before the first puck drop (a holiday-shifted week keeps the same rule against its own first night). Availability submitted after the deadline is accepted at club management’s discretion for lineup purposes but is recorded as late by the league office.","Club management is responsible for confirming that the club’s availability picture supports an eligible lineup for each scheduled game. A club that discovers an availability shortfall after the deadline must pursue a reschedule under Rule 3.3 without delay; waiting until game night is not a defense to a forfeit.","The league sets no weekly availability quota beyond the appearance cap of Rule 5.2: a player may mark himself available for as many or as few of the week's games as he can play. A club whose availability picture falls short of icing an eligible lineup must pursue a reschedule under Rule 3.3; a rostered player who does not submit availability at all in consecutive weeks is reviewed by the league office under Chapter 7. The commissioner may impose a floor for an unusual week by announcing it with that week's schedule."]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Eligible lineups","paragraphs":["A lineup is eligible when every dressed player is on the club's confirmed roster, is not under suspension, is dressed at a position permitted by his assignment under Rule 2.1, is within his weekly appearance cap under this rule, and satisfies the position requirements of the settings sheet, including a rostered goaltender. Dressing an ineligible player results in forfeiture of the game in which the player dressed, without prejudice to further discipline under Chapter 7.","Every dressed player is subject to a weekly appearance cap in the regular season, counted across the game-week in which the game falls. No active-roster player — skater or goaltender — may be dressed in more than six (6) games in a game-week. A club plays nine (9) games in a full game-week, three on each of Wednesday, Thursday and Friday. The cap is enforced when a lineup is filed: a lineup that would place a player beyond it is refused. In the playoffs the cap of Rule 8.3 applies in its place.","Training-camp players are subject to a cap of three (3) games in a game-week and may be dressed at any position regardless of their declared position. Rostered players shall be given preference over training-camp players wherever a rostered player is available for the game; a club that dresses a camp player in place of an available rostered player may be required to account for it to the league office.","Position assignment operates by group. A rostered forward may be dressed at any forward position — center, left wing or right wing — and a rostered defenseman at either defense position. A goaltender may be dressed only in goal. A training-camp player may be dressed anywhere. The league's lineup tools apply these groups when a lineup is filed, not only when it is built, and where a club dresses a camp player while a rostered player of that group is both under his weekly cap and available for the night, the tools say so before the lineup is filed.","A forfeited game counts toward these totals under Rule 3.2 and cannot be reversed to restore a player's availability."],"full":["A lineup is eligible when every dressed player is on the club’s confirmed roster, is not under suspension, is dressed at a position his assignment permits under Rule 2.1, is within his weekly game cap below, and satisfies the position requirements of the settings sheet, including a rostered goaltender. Dressing an ineligible player results in forfeiture of the game in which the player dressed, without prejudice to further discipline under Chapter 7.","Every dressed player is subject to a weekly appearance cap in the regular season and the playoffs, counted across the game week in which the game falls. A skater may be dressed in at most three (3) games in a week; a goaltender in at most six (6). The cap does not apply in the pre-season, where a club may dress any rostered or camp player as often as it needs, and where its Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position — pre-season rosters are still filling, and no club should forfeit a warm-up game for want of a body. A club plays nine (9) games in a full week — three on each of Wednesday, Thursday and Friday — so a club's nine forwards and six defensemen exactly cover its nine games, and its two goaltenders cover them with room to spare. The cap is enforced when the lineup is set: a lineup that would put a player past it is refused.","Training-camp players are capped at three (3) games a week on the same terms, and may be dressed at any position regardless of their listed one. Rostered players must be given preference over training-camp players wherever a rostered player is available for the game. A club that dresses a camp player in place of an available rostered player may be asked to account for it by the league office.","Position assignment runs by group. A rostered forward may be dressed at any forward position — center, left wing or right wing — and a rostered defenseman at either defense position. Goaltenders may be dressed only in goal. A training-camp player may be dressed anywhere. In pre-season games, so may the club’s Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager; the exception ends with the pre-season, and a saved line that carries a manager out of his group will not dress into a regular-season or playoff game. The league’s lineup tools apply these groups when a lineup is filed, not only when it is built. Where a club dresses a camp player while a rostered player of that group is both under his weekly cap and available for the night, the league's lineup tools say so before the lineup is filed.","A forfeited game counts toward these totals under Rule 3.2 and cannot be undone to restore a player's availability."]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Lineup lock","paragraphs":["Lineups lock thirty (30) minutes before the scheduled puck drop. After lock, a club may substitute a player only for a genuine emergency — a sudden disconnection, hardware failure, or personal emergency — and only with a rostered, otherwise-eligible player, reported to the league office before the game begins.","Every post-lock change carries a price: for each player changed after the lock, the club serves one (1) in-game penalty during that game. The count is per player changed per submission — swapping two players costs two penalties — and is recorded automatically when the change is submitted. Penalties owed are served as minor penalties taken in that game, verified by the officials against the box score; a club that does not serve what it owes is reviewed under Chapter 7.","Every rostered player can see the club’s set lineups for the week from My Hub — which six are dressed for each game, as management sets them — so that no player learns on game night whether he was expected. The locked lineup is the lineup of record for statistics under Chapter 6 and for eligibility review. Discrepancies between the locked lineup and the players who actually appeared are investigated by the league office, and unexplained discrepancies are treated as dressing an ineligible player under Rule 5.2."]}]},{"num":6,"title":"Statistics & verification","sections":[{"id":"6.1","title":"The official record","paragraphs":["The league's published standings, statistics, and transaction log constitute the official record of the CGHL. In any conflict between the official record and a club's own records, EA's in-game screens, or third-party trackers, the official record controls once the league office has verified the game under this chapter."]},{"id":"6.2","title":"Automatic import and evidence","paragraphs":["Scores and full box scores import automatically from the EA NHL match record after every league game — no club submits results by hand. For a game to import, it must be played in the correct private lobby (the game's 6-digit code) between the two clubs' linked EA clubs, and it must finish inside that fixture's game window — from ten (10) minutes before its scheduled puck drop to three (3) hours after. The league's poller looks only during those windows and only for the clubs scheduled in them, and the importer files only a match between the two scheduled clubs that ended inside the window; a scrimmage between the same clubs earlier that day, a rematch after the night, or a game played against a club other than the scheduled opponent is never imported as the league game. A disconnected game's replay under Rule 4.3 is merged into the sitting it continues, never filed as a new game, and a completed game is never resumed. A game that genuinely finished outside its window — a night that ran long past three hours — is filed by the league's statistics staff from the club game stats desk, not by the robot. The exceptions are the games the automatic record cannot cover: a forfeit has no EA match to import and is entered by the league office under Rule 3.2, and a lagged-out game's sessions are combined into one record through the league's stats tools — by the winning club's management or by statistics staff (Rule 4.3).","Clubs should still capture end-of-game screenshots or stream footage as evidence. If an import fails or attributes a game incorrectly, the league office replays the archived EA match data or re-opens the game; evidence resolves any dispute the archive cannot. Repeated play outside the assigned lobby — which breaks the automatic import — is subject to discipline under Chapter 7."]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Corrections and disputes","paragraphs":["A club that believes the official record is wrong must file a correction request with the league office, identifying the game and the specific entry disputed and attaching supporting evidence. The league office rules on correction requests and its statistical rulings are final, except that a ruling which itself imposes discipline may be appealed under Rule 7.6.","Statistics accrued through verified manipulation — including arranged goals, deliberate own-goals, or padding against a complicit opponent — will be struck from the record, and the players and clubs involved referred for discipline under Chapter 7."]}]},{"num":7,"title":"Discipline, suspensions & appeals","sections":[{"id":"7.1","title":"Disciplinary authority","paragraphs":["The league office holds exclusive authority to investigate suspected violations of this rulebook and to impose discipline on players, club management, and clubs. The league office may act on a filed complaint, an official’s report, or its own observation, and may review any league game in full, including recorded footage and Discord communications in league spaces.","A complaint is routed to the department that must act on it, and that department is notified the moment one is filed: complaints about conduct on the ice — and appeals of rulings — go to the officiating department, while complaints about conduct in the Discord go to the community department, whose moderators may issue warnings and escalate anything heavier. Every official can see every filing, including a complaint about a commissioner or a member of league staff. What is restricted is authority, not visibility: no official may rule on a case they filed or a case about themselves, so a complaint naming an official is decided by someone else. The substance of a complaint is never published to a staff channel — only that one was filed, and under which heading; officials open the case itself to read it.","Discipline is corrective, not merely punitive: sanctions are set to protect competitive integrity and member safety, with the player's history and the harm caused weighed in every decision."]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Classes of sanction","paragraphs":["Available sanctions include, in escalating order: formal warning, single-game suspension, multi-game suspension, forfeiture of games or statistics, suspension for the remainder of the season including playoffs, and expulsion from the league. Sanctions may be combined where a single course of conduct violates multiple rules. League staff may impose suspensions of up to ten (10) games or thirty (30) days; suspension for the remainder of the season, expulsion, and any sanction against a commissioner or staff member are imposed only by a commissioner. A staff official who believes a case warrants more than their ceiling leaves the case open and escalates it.","A suspended player may not dress, appear in a league lobby, or serve as a club's point of contact for game operations while the suspension runs. Suspensions carry across season boundaries where unserved games remain."]},{"id":"7.3","title":"In-game misconduct","paragraphs":["Conduct penalized within the game by EA's rule engine is ordinarily punished by the game itself. The league office layers supplemental discipline on top of in-game penalties where the conduct is dangerous, repeated, or targeted — the fact that a penalty was served in-game does not exhaust the league's disciplinary interest in it."]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Dangerous contact","paragraphs":["Dangerous contact is any deliberate act whose evident purpose is to injure, intimidate, or provoke rather than to play the puck. It expressly includes checking an opponent from behind — particularly into the boards or the goal frame — and all post-whistle misconduct, including hits, cross-checks, slew-foots, and goaltender contact delivered after play has been blown dead.","The baseline sanction for a verified act of dangerous contact is a two (2) game suspension. The league office adjusts upward from the baseline for aggravating factors — targeting a goaltender, repetition within the same game, prior history under this rule, or conduct that continues after a warning — and may reduce below the baseline only in documented, exceptional circumstances. Effective version 1.2 of this rulebook, the baseline is mandatory: a verified violation of this rule may not be resolved by warning alone.","Club management shares responsibility under this rule. A manager who directs, encourages, or conspicuously tolerates dangerous contact by their players is subject to discipline as if they had committed the act."]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Repeat offenses","paragraphs":["A player's full disciplinary history in the CGHL is before the league office in every matter. Repeat violations of the same rule presumptively draw a longer suspension than the prior sanction for that rule, and a pattern of violations across different rules may itself justify a season-length suspension or expulsion even where no single incident would."]},{"id":"7.6","title":"Appeals","paragraphs":["A player or club sanctioned under this chapter may appeal by filing a written appeal with the league office within forty-eight (48) hours of the sanction being posted. The appeal must identify the ruling appealed, state the grounds — factual error, misapplication of a rule, or disproportionate sanction — and attach any evidence not already before the league office. Sanctions not appealed within the 48-hour window are final.","Appeals are decided by a panel that excludes any official who imposed the original sanction. Filing an appeal does not stay a suspension: the sanction runs while the appeal is pending, and games already served are credited if the appeal succeeds. The panel may uphold, reduce, or vacate a sanction; it may increase a sanction only where the appeal itself reveals misconduct not previously known."]}]},{"num":8,"title":"Playoffs & seeding","sections":[{"id":"8.1","title":"Qualification and seeding","paragraphs":["At the conclusion of the regular season the top three (3) clubs in each division qualify for the CGHL playoffs, producing a six-club field. Clubs are seeded one through three within their own division by regular-season record. Each division plays its own bracket: the first seed receives a bye through the opening round while the second seed meets the third; the winner meets the first seed for the division championship; and the two division champions meet in the final.","Seeding is fixed when the league office certifies the final standings and does not change between rounds.","Clubs are ordered by total points earned. A regulation or overtime win is worth two (2) points, an overtime loss one (1), and a regulation loss none. The table is not adjusted for games in hand: a club that has played more games may stand higher on points alone, and the club behind it retains the opportunity to pass it by playing the games it is owed. A forfeit counts for points as an ordinary result — two points to the club awarded the win and none to the club that forfeited.","Clubs are assigned to the East and West divisions by the league office and published with the season schedule; assignments hold for the full season."],"full":["At the conclusion of the regular season, the top four (4) clubs in each division qualify for the CGHL playoffs, producing an eight-club field. Clubs are seeded one through four inside their own division by regular-season record. Each division plays its own bracket: the first seed meets the fourth and the second meets the third, the winners meet for the division title, and the two division champions meet in the final.","Seeding is fixed when the league office certifies the final standings and does not re-seed between rounds.","Clubs are ordered by total points earned. A regulation or overtime win is worth two points, an overtime loss one, and a regulation loss none. The table is not adjusted for games in hand: a club that has played more games may stand higher on points alone, and the club behind it holds the chance to pass it by playing the games it is owed. A forfeit counts for points exactly as an ordinary result, a win worth two points to the club that was ready and a loss worth none to the club that forfeited.","Clubs are assigned to the East and West divisions by the league office and published with the season schedule; assignments hold for the full season."]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Tiebreakers","paragraphs":["Ties in the standings — for division titles, playoff berths, or seeding — are broken in the following order: head-to-head record between the tied clubs; regulation wins; goal differential across all regular-season games; goals scored; and, if still tied, a one-game play-in scheduled by the league office. Forfeited games count as regulation results for all tiebreaker purposes.","A forfeit is a regulation result and never awards the overtime-loss point. Where a forfeit keeps its played score under Rule 4.3, the goals scored in it count normally toward goal differential and goals scored, even for the club that forfeited — the ruling decides the win and the loss, not the goals."]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Playoff play","paragraphs":["Playoff rounds are contested as best-of-seven series played within a single game-week in a 2-2-3 format: two games on Wednesday, two on Thursday, and up to three on Friday as required. The higher seed holds home designation for scheduling and reporting purposes. A club that wins four (4) games advances immediately, and the remaining games of the series are canceled. A division's first seed plays no series in the opening week; its postseason begins with the division championship in the following game-week.","All regular-season rules apply in the playoffs except where this rulebook or the published playoff format expressly provides otherwise. Overtime follows the continuous sudden-death format of the regular season under Rule 4.1: there are no shootouts, and a tied game plays successive overtime periods until it is decided.","A playoff appearance cap applies in place of the weekly cap of Rule 5.2: no player — skater, goaltender or training-camp player — may be dressed in more than four (4) games of a single series. The roster composition published under Rule 2.1 is sized so that a full active roster can ice an eligible lineup for all seven games of a series. Dressing a player beyond the cap is treated as dressing an ineligible player under Rule 5.2 and forfeits the game.","A player is eligible to be dressed in the playoffs only if he has appeared in at least eighteen (18) regular-season games for a club in that season, was on the club's active roster or training camp at the movement deadline under Rule 2.4, and is not under suspension for the game in question. Appearances are counted from the official record (Chapter 6) across every club the player was rostered with in the season. The league's lineup tools refuse a playoff lineup containing a player short of the floor, and Team HQ shows each club's players against it through the regular season so that management may distribute games accordingly. A goaltender signed under the emergency provision of Rule 2.4 is playoff-eligible notwithstanding the floor and his date of joining; the provision exists for that case."],"full":["Playoff rounds are contested as best-of-seven series played inside a single game week in a 2-2-3 format: two games Wednesday, two Thursday, and three Friday if they are needed. The higher seed holds home designation for scheduling and reporting purposes. A club that wins four (4) games advances immediately, and the remaining games of the series are canceled.","All regular-season rules apply in the playoffs except where this rulebook or the published playoff format expressly provides otherwise. Overtime follows the same continuous, sudden-death format as the regular season under Rule 4.1 — there are no shootouts, and a tied game plays successive overtime periods until it is decided.","The weekly appearance cap of Rule 5.2 applies in the playoffs without change: a skater may be dressed in at most three (3) games of a series, a goaltender in at most six (6). With nine forwards and six defensemen on the active roster, a club can ice a full lineup for all seven games. Dressing a player past his cap is treated as dressing an ineligible player under Rule 5.2 and forfeits the game. There is no games-played floor for playoff eligibility; the player must have been on the club's active roster at the movement deadline under Rule 2.4 and must not be under suspension for the game in question. A goaltender signed under the emergency exception of Rule 2.4 is playoff-eligible despite joining after the movement deadline — the exception exists precisely for that case."]}]},{"num":9,"title":"Awards & honors","sections":[{"id":"9.1","title":"League awards","paragraphs":["The CGHL confers end-of-season awards recognizing individual and club excellence, including most valuable player, best goaltender, best defenseman, and the league championship trophy awarded to the playoff champion. The full slate of awards for a season is published by the league office before the final week of the regular season."]},{"id":"9.2","title":"Selection and voting","paragraphs":["Individual awards are decided by a vote the league office runs among league staff and club management, informed by the official statistical record under Chapter 6. No voter may cast a first-place vote for a member of their own club. The league office publishes voting results with the award announcements.","Statistical titles — scoring leaders and goaltending leaders — are determined directly from the official record and are not subject to vote. Minimum-appearance thresholds for goaltending titles are published with the settings sheet."]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Eligibility and forfeiture of honors","paragraphs":["A player serving a suspension of season length or longer is ineligible for individual awards for that season. The league office may strip an award or honor, before or after conferral, where the underlying performance is found to have involved a verified violation of Chapter 6 or Chapter 7."]}]},{"num":10,"title":"Definitions","sections":[{"id":"10.1","title":"Terms of art","paragraphs":["As used in this rulebook: “league office” means the CGHL commissioner and staff acting in their official capacity; “club” means a member team of the CGHL together with its management and rostered players; “club management” means a club’s Owner, General Manager, and Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6); “puck drop” means the scheduled start time of a game as published on the official schedule; “dress” means to appear in a league lobby as a participant in a game; “game week” means the scheduling week as delineated on the official schedule; and “settings sheet” means the league-published game configuration document incorporated by Rule 4.1.","\"Eligible lineup\" carries the meaning given in Rule 5.2. \"Dangerous contact\" carries the meaning given in Rule 7.4. \"Official record\" carries the meaning given in Rule 6.1.","“Season format” means the model under which a season is conducted, designated by the commissioner for each season and stated at the head of this rulebook: the basic format, which is the league standard and the text of these chapters, or the full format, preserved in Appendix A. “Season settings” means the figures the commissioner determines and publishes for each season before its draft under Rules 2.1 and 2.8 — the size and composition of the active roster (which may be stated as complete lines plus additional players of any position), any training-camp limit, and the number of draft rounds. “Depth” or “depth placement” means a player placed on a club by the league office under Rule 2.8, who is the club’s player in every respect but does not count against the roster composition of Rule 2.1."]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Interpretation and amendment","paragraphs":["Chapter and section headings are for convenience and do not limit the text. Where this rulebook is silent, the league office rules in the manner that best protects competitive integrity, and such rulings have the force of the rulebook until it is amended. Time references are Eastern Time unless stated otherwise, and stated time periods include weekends and holidays.","This rulebook may be amended by the league office at any time. Amendments are published with a version number and effective date, take effect prospectively from the stated date, and do not reopen games or rulings completed under a prior version except where the amendment expressly says so."]}]}],"changelog":[{"version":"2.53","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"The banned-ability list reissued for NHL 27 (Rule 4.5). Banned at every tier: Truculence, Spark Plug, Hipster, Pressure+, Unstoppable Force and Big Rig; banned at the Red tier only: Stick ’Em Up and Big Tipper. Every pre-built loadout is banned, every special-character cosmetic is banned alongside the special-character classes, and no enforcer build may be used at either position. A player who loads into a game with a banned ability, build, loadout or cosmetic no longer forfeits the game: the game is restarted and he serves one (1) minor penalty at the start of the restarted game; a club that declines to restart forfeits (Rule 3.2). Wheels and the blanket Elite-tier ban of the NHL 26 list are withdrawn."},{"version":"2.51","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"The player-availability layout for the temporary format. Every active-roster player may be dressed in up to six (6) games a week and a training-camp player in up to three (3); the weekly availability quota of Rule 5.1 is withdrawn. In the playoffs a four (4) game cap per player per series applies to everyone, and a player must have appeared in eighteen (18) regular-season games to be dressed at all — Team HQ and each player's dashboard track the floor through the season. A roster composition may be published as complete lines plus additional players of any position (Season 1: two full lines plus three, fifteen players, management inside), and training camp is unlimited unless a limit is published (Rules 2.1, 5.1, 5.2, 8.3; Chapter 0; Rule 10.1)."},{"version":"2.50","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"Season settings, and a formal register. The size and composition of the active roster (Rule 2.1) and the number of draft rounds (Rule 2.8) are no longer fixed figures in the text: the commissioner determines both for each season and publishes them before that season's draft, and the draft pay scale is stated as a formula on the $250,000 increment from the $750,000 minimum, so the book stays true whatever figures a season carries. Season 1's settings are being finalized and will be published before draft night; the figures in force appear on the Seasons page, in the draft room and in Team HQ. The basic-format chapters (0, 2.1–2.5, 2.8, 2.9, 5.2, 8.1, 8.3) are rewritten in the formal, third-person register of a league constitution — defined terms, figures with words, shall/may/must — with the explanatory passages of Chapter 0 retained as commentary. Appendix A is unchanged."},{"version":"2.48","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"The season format. The league now runs one of two formats, set per season. The BASIC format is the standard and the text of the book from Season 1: no pre-season and no appearance requirement — everyone registered by the Thursday cutoff is in the draft; a fifteen-round draft in a random snake order paying $750,000 in round fifteen up to $4,250,000 in round one; an eighteen-man roster of nine forwards, six defensemen and three goaltenders with the Owner, GM and AGM inside it; every player capped at three games a week and three games a series; a $50,000,000 hard cap; a six-week regular season with the movement deadline after week four; players-only trades; one-season contracts with no extensions and no held rights, everyone re-entering the draft each season; no free-agency week, with waived players signable from the draft's conclusion; and a six-club playoff — top three per division, the division winners resting through the opening round, three best-of-seven rounds over three weeks. The FULL format — pre-season and loans, fourteen linear rounds, 9/6/2, goaltenders six a week, $40M, eight weeks, pick trading, multi-season deals, extensions and rights, top four per division — is preserved word for word in Appendix A for when the league grows. Season 1 is re-spaced to the basic calendar: sign-ups close Thursday September 17 at 11:59 PM ET, the draft is Saturday September 19, puck drop Wednesday September 23, the deadline after week four, playoffs from November 4 (Chapters 0, 2, 5, 8; Rules 1.1, 2.1–2.5, 2.8, 2.9, 3.1, 5.2, 8.1, 8.3, 10.1)."},{"version":"2.47","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"The Discord-departure rule is back. A registered player who leaves the league Discord before being placed on a club has the sign-up withdrawn automatically after about a day's grace, archived by the league office with the player told on the site how to come back — exactly as Rule 1.1 read before v2.43 retired it. Rejoining restores nothing by itself; signing up again is a fresh registration under the normal draft-eligibility rules, and players already on a roster are never touched. Restored at the league's direction one day after it was removed (Rule 1.1, §0.3)."},{"version":"2.46","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"Sign-ups stay open through the pre-season, and the draft-eligibility minimum is three games. The sign-up deadline — the draft-eligibility cutoff — is now 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft instead of the Monday before the pre-season, so anyone who registers during the pre-season is still in the draft pool (Rules 0.3, 1.1, 2.8; Season 1: Thursday, September 24). A randomly assigned first-year now needs three (3) pre-season appearances rather than five to remain draft-eligible, which a Thursday sign-up can still reach on the final Friday (Rules 0.4, 2.8); returning players and management stay exempt, and management's obligation to spread ice time stands. Player overalls settle in step: a rating opens at 70 and blends onto the real computed number over the first three games instead of five, and stops reading as provisional at three."},{"version":"2.45","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"Club management may @ freely in its own room. A GM tagging the players he is dressing in #stars was blocked by the server's generic spam filter, which treats a mention-heavy message as spam. The four front-office roles (Owner, General Manager, Assistant General Manager and CGHL Management) are now exempt from both mention-shaped filters, and every club room is exempt from the mention-spam rule outright — a private room of about twenty, with nobody outside the club reachable from it. Re-checked on every Discord sync so it cannot drift back (Rule 1.3)."},{"version":"2.44","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"Two changes for the whole roster. (1) The lineups your management sets for the week are now visible to every rostered player: a new Lineups page under My Hub lists each of the club's games this week with the six who are dressed, and the dashboard shows at a glance which games you are dressed for (Rule 5.3). The builder itself stays a Team HQ tool. (2) Weekly availability is answered per game. Each night keeps its own section with its three games, and every game gets Available or Not Available; the optional note per night stays for anything else you want your GM to know. Management's club grid shows one mark per game, and the lineup builder checks the answer for the exact game it is dressing, so an older per-night answer still counts (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"2.43","dateIso":"2026-09-14","summary":"Three club-facing changes. (1) A club's management can rally its room: every club role is now mentionable, so an @Club ping reaches the club's members — in the team channel and in any channel a manager can post in. Server-wide @everyone / @here pings stay reserved to the league office (Rule 1.3). (2) Leaving the Discord no longer withdraws a sign-up. Discord membership is still required to register, but a registered player keeps their place on the sign-up board and in the draft pool whether or not they stay in the server; the old automatic-withdrawal rule and its day of grace are retired (Rule 1.1, §0.3). The office keeps a manual, archived tool for genuine departures. (3) Pre-season loans are uncapped. A randomly assigned pre-season player rides the active roster as a loan and counts against neither the nine forwards / six defensemen / two goaltenders nor any roster limit, so a club can hold as many as it is assigned and every registered player gets a club for the pre-season — the assignment fills each club's open seats evenly first, then loans the rest out evenly (Rules 2.1, 0.4). Team HQ's roster page shows the loan pool as its own tally, separate from the 9/6/2 shape. (4) The weekly availability deadline moved from Sunday 8:00 PM ET to 7:30 PM ET on the day the game week starts — Wednesday in a normal week, 90 minutes before the first puck drop (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"2.42","dateIso":"2026-09-14","summary":"The front office hears every roster move, and pre-season loans are set apart in Team HQ. Every signing, waiver, release, trade step (offered, accepted, declined, withdrawn, reversed), contract offer and answer, pre-season loan, squad move and trade-block flag now reaches the club's whole management — Owner, GM and AGM — as a site notification and as a post in the club's private Discord room, the moment it happens. The person who made the move sees his own copy without a chime. Bulk placements (the pre-season random assignment, late sign-ups, post-draft placement, the loan release) post one summary per club naming the players. The public feeds — the transaction wire, #trade-block, #management-moves — are unchanged. On Team HQ's roster page, randomly assigned pre-season players sit in their own block below the club's contracted players, marked as loans, with the position they registered shown when they are filling another seat for the pre-season (Rule 0.4)."},{"version":"2.41","dateIso":"2026-09-13","summary":"The roster shape is by position group (Rule 2.1). A club's seventeen active players are nine forwards (centers and wings in any mix), six defensemen (either side, any mix) and two goaltenders — only the goaltender is locked to an exact position, because it is the one group a build cannot cross (Rule 4.5). A club short of left defensemen carries an extra right defenseman; a club short of centers, an extra wing. The database, the roster page, the pre-season random assignment, post-draft placement and the draft all count by group now — a club with three centers can draft a fourth to fill a wing — and a camp swap matches the group, not the exact position. Rules 0.4, 0.6, 2.8, 5.2 and 8.3 say the same."},{"version":"2.40","dateIso":"2026-09-13","summary":"Pre-season random assignment fills every open roster seat before anyone goes to camp (Rule 0.4). The league office's random assignment now works in passes across the whole sign-up pool: every player to a club with room at his registered position first; then anyone left into an open seat in his position group; then into any open seat at all; and only when every active seat in the league is filled are the rest placed in training camp, evenly and at random. Before this, a player whose position was full at every club went straight to camp while other positions sat empty — on today's pool that left six clubs a defenseman short with thirteen players unplaced. A pre-season loan out of position is for the pre-season only and does not change a player's registered position. Late sign-ups placed during the pre-season follow the same order; post-draft placement is unchanged (own position, or camp)."},{"version":"2.39","dateIso":"2026-09-12","summary":"Pre-season flexibility for management (Rules 0.4, 2.1 and 5.2). In pre-season games a club’s Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position — a goaltending Owner can play the wing, a defenseman GM can take center — so a club can fill its bench and get as many players into games as possible. The exception ends with the pre-season: a saved line carrying a manager out of his group dresses into pre-season games only, and the lineup builder says so. Alongside it, position groups (forwards at forward, defensemen on defense, goaltenders in goal; training-camp players anywhere) are now checked by the league’s database when a lineup or a line is filed, not only by the builder that drew it."},{"version":"2.38","dateIso":"2026-09-12","summary":"Front-office seats are the Owner’s alone, and the Owner sets each manager’s access (Rule 2.6). Only a club’s Owner may nominate a General Manager or Assistant GM, and only the Owner may remove one — at once, from Team HQ, without a league-office step; the seat falls vacant, the manager’s management contract ends with it, the move posts to the transaction wire, the removed manager is told, and the league office is notified. A GM or AGM nominates and removes nobody; the league office keeps its own power to seat and unseat. New under Team HQ → Management, for the Owner only: management permissions. For every front-office page — roster, lineup builder, schedule picks, Trade Hub, free agents, draft — each seat gets full access, Owner approves (the manager sees the page, but every move on it is queued for the Owner and made only on approval, through the usual checks), or hidden (the page is withheld and its moves refused); the game stats desk is open or hidden only, since box-score repairs are reviewed by the statistics staff. The description the Owner approves is built by the league from the move itself, never typed by the manager. The default is full access everywhere except the Management page itself, which only the Owner sees unless the Owner opens it — so nothing else changes for a club that never sets it. Approvals live on the same page, with a note either way; a manager can withdraw a queued move; a move that can no longer be made when approved fails loudly rather than half-applies. The database enforces every mode, not just the page. Every member of management — Owner, GM and AGM alike — now has the same Team HQ; before this, a manager seated or removed while signed in kept the old hub until a hard refresh, and it now follows the seat on its own."},{"version":"2.37","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"The stats import keeps to the game window and the scheduled matchup (Rule 6.2). A box score now imports automatically only when the match was played between the two clubs scheduled against each other and finished inside that fixture's game window — ten minutes before puck drop to three hours after. The poller asks EA only while a window is open and only for the clubs playing in it; the importer refuses everything else and archives it. Before this, a match between two league clubs anywhere on the calendar day (or the day either side) could be filed as their league game — a scrimmage that afternoon, a rematch after the night. The importer now also follows Rule 4.3 as written: a disconnected game's replay — the whole game, or one period — is merged into the abandoned sitting as one record, and a completed game is never resumed; on a playoff night that plays the same clubs twice, each match files on the slot it was played in. (The merge had never fired on real games: it measured EA's clock in the wrong units.) A game that finished outside its window is filed by the statistics staff from the club game stats desk."},{"version":"2.36","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"The Season 2 calendar, and the pre-season is uncapped everywhere it is described. Season 2 now exists on the site, spaced from Season 1's final playoff game (Fri Jan 15, 2027) exactly as Rule 0.2 onward describes: dark weeks Jan 16–29, owner applications by Fri Jan 22, sign-ups by Fri Jan 29 at 8:00 PM ET, pre-season Wed Feb 3 – Fri Feb 12, draft Sat Feb 13 at 7:00 PM ET, free agency Sun Feb 14 – Sun Feb 21, puck drop Wed Feb 24, eight game weeks stepping over Easter week, regular season ending Fri Apr 23, playoffs from Wed Apr 28 with the final the week of May 12. Its sign-ups open automatically at Season 1's movement deadline (Rule 1.1); until then the site, the Discord roles and every league-office routine keep treating Season 1 as the season in play — the current season is now defined once, everywhere, as the season that is active or, failing that, the earliest one not yet complete. Rule 0.4 says the pre-season opens on the first Wednesday after the sign-up deadline (it is two days later only in a first season). Rule 2.1 now says outright that the training-camp three-games-a-week cap belongs to the regular season and the playoffs; Rule 5.2 already exempted the pre-season, and the lineup tools, the line planner and Team HQ now agree with it (a goaltender may cover every line in the pre-season). Behind the site: box scores are imported from the always-on bot server, whose address EA serves, with the old Netlify poller kept as a fallback."},{"version":"2.35","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"Audit corrections. The book now says what the binding rules and the site already did: playoff series caps are three games for a skater and six for a goaltender (0.8 now matches 8.3); a trade is official the moment the second club accepts it, and the transactions department may reverse it afterward (2.3; 2.8 now cites 2.3); the pre-season opens the Wednesday after the sign-up deadline (0.4), and the pre-season table is one basis for the draft order, not the basis (0.4); registration for the next season opens automatically at the movement deadline (0.2, 0.3); how a former club exercises its rights over a restricted free agent is set by the league office before the first restricted class appears (2.2); a dead clause about a late registrant being signed through free agency is gone (0.3, 2.2); a short-handed club is refilled by automatic placement, not through a free-agent process that is closed most of the year (2.1); the loadout lists were written against NHL 26 and apply until reissued (4.5). Behind the book: pre-season loans are released when the last pre-season game closes by any result, a forfeit included, and again as a safety net the moment the draft opens; the pre-season random assignment now runs inside the database, position by position; the free-agent board lists only players whose deals have ended; the draft-night pick modal puts eligible players first; a late sign-up is labeled as awaiting placement rather than as needing pre-season games; the register page names the deadline and says what changes once it has passed; and the pre-season and draft-night Discord announcements no longer describe the abolished no-minimum and rookie-bidding rules."},{"version":"2.34","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"Contract extensions and the cap year (Rules 2.2 and 2.5). A club can now re-sign a player it already holds: at any point in the final season of his deal — from the day that season's free agency opens, as in the NHL — management offers him a new contract from Team HQ, or he opens with his own number from his dashboard, and the two negotiate as in free agency. The cap year runs from one free-agency opening to the next, so an accepted extension is a signed deal for the following season: nothing changes this season, and it is checked against next season's space — every deal already signed for it plus the front office — which is the space the club will have once its expiring deals turn over. Every signing, now or for a future season, is refused outright if it would not fit; there is no exception. Team HQ now shows each club its committed money and cap space for this season and the next three, from the same arithmetic the checks use. A club keeps the exclusive right to re-sign a player whose deal just ended until the new season's free agency opens; a deal signed in that stretch is for the season under way and takes effect at once. An extension follows the player through a trade and is voided by a waiver. No club other than the one holding his deal may approach him until it has ended and free agency has opened. Until now there was no way to offer a contract to a rostered player at all, and accepting any future-season deal quietly expired the player's current one. Also: contract terms run one to three seasons everywhere — the offer forms and the database allowed four, which Rule 2.5 never did."},{"version":"2.33","dateIso":"2026-09-10","summary":"Draft and player-movement rulings. The entry draft is fourteen (14) rounds (Rule 2.8), up from ten; under the pay-by-round scale that makes round one $4,000,000 and a full class $33,250,000 — $750,000 under the cap with management seated — and the scale itself is unchanged. No club drafts without a full front office: the draft will not start while any club is missing an Owner, GM or AGM (Rules 2.6, 2.8). Rookie bidding is abolished (Rule 2.2): ten minutes after the draft concludes, every registered player still without a club — undrafted or short of five pre-season games — is placed on one by the league office at the league minimum, in random order; no club chooses, and no rookie goes to free agency (Rule 2.8). The ten-minute placement the book has promised since v2.22 is now real. Free agency is for players whose contracts have ended; a player in the final season of his deal may talk only to his own club until it opens (Rule 2.2). A drafted or placed player's first contract runs one season (Rule 2.5). The sign-up deadline is now an explicit test of draft eligibility rather than a side effect of when a player was placed (Rule 2.8). Season 1's order was drawn at random, as Rule 2.8 provides for a first season, and holds in every round. Housekeeping: a contract with no club now ends when the player is waived or a management seat is vacated, instead of lingering and mispricing a later draft pick; eighteen such contracts were closed. Separately, members can now switch the Discord account the league follows from their own settings page; site roles move at once and server roles follow."},{"version":"2.32","dateIso":"2026-09-09","summary":"Position changes now have a deadline (new Rule 2.9): 11:59 PM Eastern on the Tuesday before the first pre-season game — Tuesday September 15 for Season 1. After it, no position-change request may be filed and none may be approved; one already in the queue may still be declined so nothing is left hanging, and the commissioner keeps a narrow power to correct an office error. The deadline is derived from the published schedule rather than stored as a date, so re-spacing the pre-season moves it automatically instead of leaving a stale rule behind. Previously there was no deadline at all: a request could be filed and granted at any point in the season, which would have changed a position the pre-season assignment and every club's draft board were built on. The rule is enforced in the database on both filing and approval, and the request form now says when it closes."},{"version":"2.31","dateIso":"2026-09-09","summary":"Every salary in the league now sits on one $250,000 lattice above the $750,000 minimum (Rule 2.5). Previously only rookie bidding used that step: the rulebook named the minimum and nothing else, and the offer forms accepted any $50,000 figure, so $1,850,000 was a legal contract. The single step now governs draft pay, rookie bidding, free-agent offers, counters and revisions alike, and an off-lattice figure is refused where it is typed. A figure below the minimum used to be silently raised to it on the club's behalf; it is now refused too, so the number a club sends is always the number the player is asked to accept. Drafted players are also now paid by round rather than by pick (Rule 2.8): the last round pays the league minimum and each round above adds one step, so this season's ten-round draft runs $750,000 in round ten up to $3,000,000 in round one, every pick in a round carries the same cap hit, and a full class costs $18,750,000. The scale applies however a pick is made — by the club, by the league office, or by the clock expiring onto the club's own board. Adopted by a vote of the club owners."},{"version":"2.30","dateIso":"2026-09-02","summary":"Squad changes are unlimited (Rule 2.1): management may move a player between the active roster and training camp freely, as often as it likes, in either direction, throughout the season — including after the movement deadline, which Rule 2.4 now states explicitly never restricted these internal moves. The three-changes-a-season cap is abolished; no club had used a change when it was lifted. The active-roster shape and the three-player camp limit are unchanged and still enforced on every move. Team HQ no longer shows changes remaining or a locked squad button, and the swap picker lists every player at the position."},{"version":"2.29","dateIso":"2026-09-02","summary":"A training-camp player's salary now counts against his club's salary cap (Rules 2.1 and 2.5), reversing the exemption introduced in v2.24. Camp is unchanged in every other respect: a club may still carry three camp players beyond its seventeen active spots, they may still be dressed at any position, and they still do not consume an active roster spot. What changes is only the money — parking a salary in camp is no longer free, and the cap check at every roster move, trade and signing now counts it."},{"version":"2.28","dateIso":"2026-08-29","summary":"Free agency becomes a negotiation, as Rule 2.2 always described it: a club offers terms, the player accepts, counters, or declines, and the signing takes effect the instant he accepts — the league office confirms nothing and has no say in the move. Until now the site signed players unilaterally, first come first served, with no offer and no acceptance. The transactions department may afterwards send a completed trade back where it is not a natural hockey deal (Rule 2.4), returning every player and pick to its original club with a reason both clubs can read. The weekly appearance cap no longer applies in the pre-season (Rule 5.2): at first-season roster sizes the cap made most pre-season games impossible to ice legally, and every unfilled game is a forfeit."},{"version":"2.27","dateIso":"2026-08-25","summary":"The entry draft runs in the same order every round — the snake is abolished (Rule 2.8, Chapter 0.5). A fifth order method is added: an NHL-style draft lottery drawn from the previous season, with weighted odds for the top two picks among non-playoff clubs and playoff clubs following in reverse order of finish; a first season, having no history, uses a pure random shuffle instead. Rule 2.8 also now states what was already league practice: draft picks are tradeable assets under the ordinary trade rules, before and during the draft, with a traded pick belonging to its new club the moment the deal is accepted."},{"version":"2.26","dateIso":"2026-08-24","summary":"Two clarifications aligning the rulebook with how the league actually runs. Rule 2.6: management cap hits are Owner $0, General Manager $3,000,000, Assistant General Manager $3,000,000 — the GM line now matches the season configuration and the owners' briefing. Rule 2.1: an active player may be dressed at any position within his group (a forward at center or either wing, a defenseman at either side), never a skater in goal or a goaltender out of it, matching lineup enforcement; training-camp players remain fully flexible."},{"version":"2.25","dateIso":"2026-08-19","summary":"Rule 2.2 defines two classes of free-agent rights on the NHL's model. A player who has never completed a season on a roster is unrestricted. Once he has served a club, his rights stay with that club when the contract ends and he is a restricted free agent until he accrues four (4) off-seasons of service, after which he is unrestricted for good. Every player is unrestricted in Season 1 because the league has no service history yet. Rookie, Free Agent and Restricted Free Agent are now Discord roles, granted and revoked automatically from the same rule."},{"version":"2.24","dateIso":"2026-08-18","summary":"Rule 2.5 states plainly that a training-camp player's salary does not count against the salary cap, and Rule 2.1 says the same where camp is defined. Camp already sat outside the seventeen active spots; it now sits outside the payroll with them, and a camp player's salary begins to count only on call-up. The site had been charging camp salaries to the cap and counting camp players against the seventeen, so clubs were shown less room than they had."},{"version":"2.23","dateIso":"2026-08-18","summary":"Rule 2.6 now states plainly that a club seat is held by one person, that one person holds at most one club seat league-wide, and that an appointment through the application process into a seat that is already held is refused rather than applied. Previously such an approval overwrote the sitting manager without notice. The seat is vacated first; the league office keeps its own power to seat and unseat management directly. A nomination that cannot be seated stays open, the league office and the nominating club are told why, and the Review Board flags it before the deciding vote is cast."},{"version":"2.22","dateIso":"2026-08-12","summary":"Season 1 rules package. The pre-season now decides draft eligibility: a randomly assigned player who is not already on a club’s full-time roster must appear in at least five (5) pre-season games to stay draft-eligible, returning players and club management are exempt, and management is obligated to spread pre-season ice time so everyone who needs five games can get them. Management plays the pre-season for its own club rather than being drawn into the random assignment. Standings now run on total points earned rather than points percentage, so games in hand show in the table as they fall, and a forfeit scores for points exactly as an ordinary win or loss. Training camp is capped at three (3) players, each dressable at any position. The movement deadline lands at midnight at the end of the Friday of the deadline week. The ten-minute forfeit may now be waived, but only by a member of league staff with no involvement in the game. The banned X-Factor and build lists will be reviewed and reissued when NHL 27 releases. The playoff field expands to the top four clubs in each division, an eight-club divisional bracket."},{"version":"2.21","dateIso":"2026-08-12","summary":"Rule 1.1 closes the loop on the Discord requirement: a registered player who leaves the league Discord has their pending sign-up withdrawn automatically after about a day's grace. The registration is archived by the league office and the player is notified on the site with a path back — rejoining and signing up again is always possible and counts as a fresh registration under the normal draft-eligibility rules. Players already placed on a club are unaffected; those situations go through the league office."},{"version":"2.20","dateIso":"2026-08-12","summary":"Rule 2.6 adds the application waitlist: owner and staff applications with no open seat can be waitlisted by the league office, and when a seat opens — a resignation, a non-renewal, or a staff departure — the longest-waiting eligible application fills it automatically, in waitlist order. Eligibility is re-checked at the moment of appointment against the management-separation rule (2.7), the appointee and the commissioners are notified immediately, and the office may reverse any automatic appointment on review."},{"version":"2.19","dateIso":"2026-08-10","summary":"Rule 1.3's anti-spam gate gains a GIF carve-out: Discord's built-in GIF picker now works for everyone, including accounts that have not yet registered. Every other link from an unregistered account is refused outright — the message is blocked, which is stricter than before, when such links simply posted without a preview. Registered members are unaffected. The advertising, scam and invite filters are unchanged."},{"version":"2.18","dateIso":"2026-08-10","summary":"Rule 5.3 now prices post-lock lineup changes: one (1) in-game penalty per player changed after the thirty-minute lock, counted per submission, recorded automatically, served as minor penalties in that game and verified by the officials against the box score. Going unserved is reviewed under Chapter 7. The emergency-substitution allowance itself is unchanged — this sets its cost, so a genuine no-show swap is one penalty and wholesale post-lock rebuilds price themselves out."},{"version":"2.17","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 1.3 records #scouting-links, the club recruitment board: the whole league can read it, and only a club's Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager may post in it. Clubs post their own Discord there for recruiting; the channel is exempt from the invite-link and advertising filters because ordinary recruiting copy trips them, and because the set of people who can write in it is already the narrowest on the server."},{"version":"2.16","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 1.3 corrected on server invite links. They were blocked outright for everyone except the Commissioner and Staff, which caught club owners sharing their own team's Discord — a normal and useful thing to post. Invite links now follow the same line as every other link: sign up and you may post one; an account that has not signed up may not. Advertising and scam content are still blocked automatically for everyone, as is mention spam."},{"version":"2.15","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 3.1 now states the season length the league actually plays: eight (8) game-weeks and seventy-two (72) games per club, up from the six (6) and fifty-four (54) it had carried since v2.6. The schedule had been built to eight weeks for some time — this corrects the rulebook to match the ice, it does not change anyone's season. Rule 3.1 also now says plainly that a week skipped for an observed holiday does not shorten the season: the schedule runs a week later and the game count still holds. The figure on the Puck drop timeline is now read from the season itself rather than restated, so it cannot drift from the schedule again."},{"version":"2.14","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 3.1 and the orientation chapter now state when the playoffs open: the game week immediately after the final regular-season week. This was always the intent but was never written down, and nothing enforced it — a season whose weeks shifted because an observed holiday was skipped kept its original playoff date, which put the last regular-season week on top of the postseason. Generating the regular season now sets the playoff date from the weeks it actually laid down, and the Control Center flags the two drifting apart."},{"version":"2.13","dateIso":"2026-08-08","summary":"Rule 1.3 records the Discord anti-spam measures: posting links, images, or attachments now requires a league role — register on the site and it unlocks within seconds — new accounts must be in the server ten minutes before posting at all, and invites, advertising, and scam content are blocked automatically. Reading and chatting are unchanged."},{"version":"2.12","dateIso":"2026-08-06","summary":"Chapter 7 now states who holds which disciplinary power, matching what the system already enforces: on-ice complaints and appeals route to the officiating department, Discord-conduct complaints to the community department (warnings, escalate the rest); staff suspensions cap at 10 games or 30 days, and season-long suspension, expulsion, or any sanction against a commissioner or staff member is a commissioner's alone."},{"version":"2.11","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Rule 1.3 tightened: server-wide pings follow the Commissioner and Staff roles only. The Media role had been left able to send them on the reasoning that media-only staff do not wear the Staff role — which meant someone carrying neither Commissioner nor Staff could ping the whole server, the exact thing the rule forbids. Media no longer carries it; a staffer whose only role is their department asks the office to post."},{"version":"2.10","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Rule 7.1 now states how a complaint travels: it reaches the department that must act on it, and that department is told the moment it is filed. Every official can see every filing, including one about a commissioner or a staff member; what is restricted is authority, not visibility — no official rules on a case they filed or a case about themselves. The substance of a complaint is never posted to a staff channel, only that one was filed and under which heading."},{"version":"2.9","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Rule 1.3: @everyone and @here are now reserved to commissioners and league staff. The permission had been granted to every position role, every club-seat role, every staff department role and the server-booster role — which in practice meant every registered player could ping the entire server. It has been removed from all of them and is re-enforced on every Discord sync, so it cannot drift back."},{"version":"2.8","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Game-night law, in one pass. Late starts (Rule 3.2): one penalty at five minutes, two at eight — the ready club choosing a 5-on-3 or two 5-on-4s — and a hard, unwaivable forfeit at ten; the penalties themselves may be waived by mutual agreement, and matchmaking delay from the previous game is exempt. Disconnections (Rule 4.3) rewritten: the dropped club leaves at the next whistle with everything before it counting, reloads with a penalty timed to the drop, doubles it on a second, and a third ends the game as a forfeit loss that KEEPS its statistics and publishes as an FFL even if the forfeiting club scored more; third-period drops take the penalty five minutes earlier, and a drop in the first five minutes of the third replays one period as the third. Weekly caps corrected to the enforced numbers (Rule 5.2): skaters three, goaltenders six, camp three at any position, rostered players taking precedence. Playoffs (Rule 8.3): best-of-seven in a 2-2-3 week, first to four advancing, the weekly cap serving as the series cap, and the Rule 2.4 emergency goaltender expressly playoff-eligible. Rule 4.1 gains the lobby side-selection rule and the 20-minute clock-display note. Registration (Rule 1.1): players may withdraw their own sign-up until registration ends, and the next season's registration opens automatically when the movement deadline passes. The rulebook file itself was consolidated from its amendment chain into a single current text."},{"version":"2.7","dateIso":"2026-08-03","summary":"The roster rework. The active roster is now a fixed seventeen — 3 centers, 3 left wings, 3 right wings, 3 left defensemen, 3 right defensemen, 2 goaltenders — and training camp is unlimited (Rule 2.1). Weekly game caps arrive: camp players dress in up to 3 games a week at any position, active players up to 6, goaltenders up to 9 (Rule 5.2). Every minimum-games rule is abolished — the five-game draft threshold (Rule 2.8) and the 30% playoff floor (Rule 8.3) are gone; every registered player is draft-eligible and every rostered player is playoff-eligible. Random placement of new players now closes at the movement deadline (Rules 2.2, 2.4). The playoff series cap is restated: four games per series for skaters, with goaltenders exempt and able to play all seven if needed (Rule 8.3)."},{"version":"2.6","dateIso":"2026-08-02","summary":"The playing week is now three nights — Wednesday, Thursday and Friday — three games a night, nine games a week, for the same fifty-four games per club over six game-weeks instead of nine (Rule 3.1, Chapter 0). The weekly availability floor scales with it: six of the week’s nine games including all three games of at least two nights, up from four of six including one night (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"2.5","dateIso":"2026-07-30","summary":"Rule 2.7 tightened again: Media holds NO ballot. Not on staff votes of any kind, not on rule changes, and not on season awards — previously they could still vote on anything scoped to media. A vote can bend toward the club a media staffer runs, and Media is the one department allowed to run a club, so the department now reports on the league rather than deciding for it. Media staff still read every tally. Commissioners are unaffected. Enforced on the staff vote, the award ballot, and the eligible-voter count so turnout can't be skewed by a ballot nobody can cast."},{"version":"2.4","dateIso":"2026-07-29","summary":"Rule 2.7 tightened: Media is an exclusive staff department. Someone on Media holds Media and nothing else, and votes only on media topics — not on whole-office questions. In exchange Media stays the one department that may also hold a club's Owner, GM or AGM seat, because a media staffer decides nothing about any club. Want a second department? You give up Media and the club seat. Commissioners are exempt throughout — the office carries every department and every vote. Enforced by the site at every write path, including the staff vote."},{"version":"2.3","dateIso":"2026-07-29","summary":"Rule 2.7 amended: Media is the one staff department that may also hold a club's Owner, GM, or AGM seat. Media rules on nothing — no disputes, discipline, applications, officiating, transactions or draft — so a media staffer running a club has no decision to make about their own team. The exception is Media-alone: taking on a second department means giving up the club seat, a seated member joining the staff may be given Media and nothing else, and it never applies to commissioners. Enforced by the site, both directions."},{"version":"2.2","dateIso":"2026-07-24","summary":"Rookie free agency runs as a live auction (Rule 2.2): bids on an undrafted five-game rookie start at $750K and rise in $250K increments, and every bid resets a 12-hour clock — the high bid when the clock expires wins, and the bid amount becomes the contract (previously only the first bid started the clock). Open free agency is clarified: for every other available player, clubs send custom-salary offers and the player chooses, expected in good faith to take the strongest offer — signing a lower one (to play with friends) isn’t forbidden but isn’t to be abused."},{"version":"2.1","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"Terminology corrected across the rulebook: the rules referred to a club “captain,” a role the league does not use. A club is run by its management — Owner, General Manager, and Assistant GM (Rule 2.6) — so protests and paperwork (1.3), trades (2.3), reschedules (3.3), game-code release (4.2), lobby-failure evidence (4.3), availability (5.1), discipline (7.1, 7.4), and award voting (9.2) now cite club management, and the definitions (10.1) replace “captain” with “club management.”"},{"version":"2.0","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"New Rule 4.5 — Positional builds and X-Factor restrictions. To keep games decided by skill rather than loadout, the league bans a defined set of X-Factor traits (Truculence, Big Tipper, Hipster, Pressure+, Spark Plug, Unstoppable, Big Rig, Wheels) and every Elite-tier trait, bars all special-character player classes and special or mascot characters, and locks each position to a legal set of builds: forwards may not use the Enforcer build (or any defenseman build), and defensemen may not use the Enforcer Defenseman build (or any forward build) — Power Forward and Defensive Defenseman are permitted. Icing a banned build or ability makes the lineup ineligible and forfeits the game (Rule 5.2). The full list is maintained by the league office with the settings sheet (Rule 4.1)."},{"version":"1.9","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"Rule 2.7 corrected: a commissioner may serve as league staff — the two offices work together. The separation that still holds is between the league office (commissioners and staff) and a club's front office (Owner, GM, AGM): no one holds a seat in both at the same time, in either direction. The staff application reflects this — anyone currently owning or managing a club must step down from that seat before joining the staff, and vice versa."},{"version":"1.8","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"The salary cap is stated as what it is — a season setting the commissioner controls — and Rule 2.5 now shows the live figure for the current season rather than a number frozen into the text. Rule 2.1 gains the operative form of the training-camp game cap: because the two game nights together run past three games, a camp player may be dressed on only one of them, and the lineup builder now enforces it."},{"version":"1.7","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"Roster structure fixed at fifteen players — a twelve-player pro roster of 2 goaltenders, 4 defensemen and 6 forwards, plus 3 training-camp players (Rule 2.1). Position assignments are now binding: defensemen play defense and forwards play forward, and only a training-camp player may fill any position. Training-camp players are capped at 3 games a week (Rules 2.1, 5.2), and a player may swap between the pro roster and training camp no more than 3 times a season — all of it enforced in Team HQ. The entry draft is fixed at ten rounds (Rule 2.8). Free agency now runs on two tracks (Rule 2.2): undrafted rookies who met the five-game pre-season minimum are bid on, with a 12-hour countdown starting at the first bid, while returning players whose contracts expired negotiate open re-signings with club management."},{"version":"1.6","dateIso":"2026-07-19","summary":"Season 1 will be played on EA Sports NHL 27, which releases September 11, 2026 — ahead of the September 16 pre-season and the October 7 puck drop. Rule 4.1 and the site now name NHL 27 as the official title; the game settings themselves are unchanged (World of Chel 6v6: three 4-minute periods, continuous full-length 5-on-5 sudden-death overtime, no shootout)."},{"version":"1.5","dateIso":"2026-07-18","summary":"Annual registration made universal: a contract does not replace signing up (Rule 2.5). A contracted player who does not register cannot play, while the full salary counts against the club's cap as dead money; if the club's ownership changes while the player is still unregistered past the sign-up deadline, the contract is voided and the player is suspended through the deal's final season. Registering at any time returns a contracted player directly to their club at no net cap change. Automatic placement explicitly never applies to contracted, suspended, or banned players (Rule 2.2); the season walk-through was updated to match (Chapter 0.3, 0.9)."},{"version":"1.4","dateIso":"2026-07-18","summary":"Game settings updated to World of Chel: three 4-minute periods with continuous, full-length 5-on-5 sudden-death overtime and no shootout (Rule 4.1; playoffs conformed in Rule 8.3). Added Chapter 0, a full step-by-step walk-through of how the season works and why. Codified the entry draft (Rule 2.8), the separation of league staff and commissioners from club management (Rule 2.7), and automatic placement of unrostered registrants onto short-handed clubs (Rule 2.2, reconciled with the movement deadline in Rule 2.4). Corrected the season length to nine game-weeks / 54 games (Rule 3.1), expanded the disconnect procedure (Rule 4.3), and set a weekly availability floor (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"1.3","dateIso":"2026-07-15","summary":"Chapter 2 expanded to codify the salary cap and contract system (Rule 2.5) and the Owner/GM/AGM management structure (Rule 2.6): the $65M hard cap, one-to-three-season contracts, the $750K minimum, waiver salary reinstatement, $0 Owner/GM contracts, and the $3M assistant-manager contract. Seeding now states the points-percentage standard (Rule 8.1)."},{"version":"1.2","dateIso":"2026-07-01","summary":"Overtime format changed to a 5-minute 3-on-3 sudden-death period followed by a shootout (Rule 4.1). Rule 7.4 (Dangerous contact) strengthened: the 2-game baseline suspension is now mandatory for verified violations and may not be resolved by warning alone."},{"version":"1.1","dateIso":"2026-06-10","summary":"Clarified disconnect and rejoin procedure (Rule 4.3): individual drops play on with rejoin at the next stoppage; full lobby failures resume from the recorded score and clock where feasible."},{"version":"1.0","dateIso":"2026-05-20","summary":"Initial publication of the CGHL Official Rulebook for Season 1."}]},"rankings":{"intro":"","entries":[]},"ops":{"complaints":[]},"awards":{"threeStars":[],"potw":[]}};
